   451	### New canon minted (author's list)
   452	- **Oxhollow's grain cart (flag).** Since his carts "go round", the keeper sends one to Thornwater for grain on the 85th, eight days out and eight home. Its carter is his sister's son: a long, sleepy-faced young man with very large feet and two grey mules, who says about forty words in six days. The three boxes ride his bed from Oxhollow to Thornwater (85th–93rd) for nothing, because the five walk his road in front of him. No paper, no line. He rests the 91st at Lowmarch and loads grain at Thornwater on the 94th.
   453	- **Lira's boot.** The carter's spare pair, each "as long as Cael's forearm"; he will not split a pair; two marks. She packs the right with a torn shirt over her taped foot; the left hangs from the cart's tail, is left at Thornwater's barn door by the carter, and goes east on Oryn's mule "against the other one going".
   454	- **The road Oxhollow–Lowmarch (flag):** south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, small fords, halting-grounds; on the fourth day two hundred yards of moss crossed by a causeway of laid logs (the carter's father's), about one log in six rotten at the heart. It reaches Lowmarch's river on the west bank and crosses by the ferry.
   455	- **Teague's crew on a road:** single file, a crew's length apart; no fire at any halt; chock-stones left as found; the mules at the windward end.
   456	- **The ferryman's rope (flag):** sixty yards of four-strand, laid up by himself. He will take no silver ("a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from"); he will have a rope when they have one. Entered by Karis as owed, with no price.
   457	- **The two HOLD slips (flag):** one bark and one shingle; a bull to be brought down off top grazing before the snow, and a ford to be staked. Each hold "asked who". They will wait a fortnight for the five and then have Teague. Cael tells Pike on the 91st not to keep them past the fortnight. **They are still on the oak at the end of the movement.**
   458	- **How the posting came (flag):** by the last mail-ferry, to the landing broker, sealed, with a hundred marks in a bag and "a line of direction"; the broker's clerk brought it up the next morning. Pike said it twice that day to forty people, nobody scratched, and he has kept it under his stone since ("A thing on a tack is a thing I'm offering."). The clerk is the hirer's witness: whatever is brought down is to be seen at the landing, marked, and sent west by the next boat. He does not know what the things are or who wants them.
   459	- **The posting as an object (flag), by Seln:** cream paper of an office's weight; a margin pricked with a wheel; "Recovery." set alone as a heading; the sum in words and again in a bracket; the place described and not named.
   460	- **The wall on the 90th:** the five are tenth; one of the two crews they passed is four people, none young, one with a wooden foot, who brought a mule string through the cold road's pass and lost none.
   461	- **Pike's rule for a job with nothing at the end of it:** "I write what was done… if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut."
   462	- **Eleven witnesses on the 91st:** Teague and his three; the Oxhollow carter; the hide-woman; the old carter under the ash; the shingle pair; the standing-money boy; the broker's clerk.
   463	- **Teague (flag):** he has been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and has never put it to the proof. He once saw a surveyor's kit, "years ago and a long way west". His reason for not taking the paper is his three; his sense that the paper is wrong has no reason, and he tells nobody.
   464	- **The registry's printed sheet (flag):** east of the Line it shows a river or two without their bends, hills drawn alike, and seven dashed roads with no names that run out into white and stop; the third from the top crosses the first water (no ferry, no Lowmarch) and ends three fingers east of it, at about Thornwater's fence or an hour beyond. **"Void"** (flag): Seln says that in a registry's usage an entry is void when nothing is written against it, and supposes that a carter was once shown such a sheet.
   465	- **Three blank circles on the carter's map (flag; see owner flag 4):** the bend east of Thornwater, and two round blanks that Karis had read as thrift, one in his hill-hatching a day south of Lowmarch and a little aside, one far to the south-east among his water-strokes with no road within a day. She rings all three in pencil and writes *guess* beside two. The hide is "a map of days" and will not take a straightedge.
   466	- **Oryn at Thornwater (flag):** she mends the carter's split palm on a plank inside the west gate before she eats ("That's one."). Her changed plan: she had meant to sit at this gate's table until the 99th; she walks the Fallow road in those days, the same six gates waiting the same days; Thornwater's bench "goes short" and is to be paid on her way back. She has been round the ring "thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts" (her round goes on east by that road). The carters' saying, as given: *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.*, first heard at the first gate she ever mended at.
   467	- **Karis's reserve:** her first point since the 78th is set on a lamp-wick in Thornwater's yard on the 93rd, at Oryn's bidding; "three parts full".
   468	- **The long cord:** two box-ropes spliced by Brom, stretched from a fence-post, marked off with red wool against the short cord. **Its length is never stated**, nor is Karis's stride.
   469	- **The Fallow road (flag):** leaves Thornwater by the east gate; forks an hour out (the left branch to a hold up the left-hand water; the right over a low place in the hills); two days due east through tall pale ungrazed grass. The holds either side call it fallow and give no reason.
   470	- **The Ring's ground (flag; one picture for chapters 27–30):** at the crest of a long rise the ruts swing out to the north and come back to their line on the far side, keeping about forty yards from the line all the way round. The line crosses the old straight line of the road **eighty of Cael's strides** beyond the turn; Karis's grey stone marks it (the circle's western point). The camp is at the turn. The floor is three hundred yards from the turn. The perimeter is walked from the stone northward, with the ring on the right hand; Brom cuts a turf at each cord; the constant offset is **six feet and a half** to the cord.
   471	- **The stake:** a square iron bar as thick as two fingers, a hand's length clear of the turf, its head beaten flat, a boot's width from Karis's third station; the registry's stamp a thumb's breadth below the head, with *thirty-one* punched under it. Not touched or drawn.
   472	- **Hound sign outside:** three runs through the grass within twenty yards of each other on the eastern side, crossing the line both ways; none on the north side.
   473	- **What each of the five reports inside** (beyond the protected lines): Karis can see where sixteen points would go and "can't find the hand to reach with"; this is not being empty ("reserve present, sigil gone"). Brom: "There's no next room"; a hundredweight weighs the same. Seln: "It is not there to be asked." Lira: a yard and a half, and no crack. Oryn: the surface count stops at "Four—"; returns at the stride; and stops when she alone is inside with Brom's wrist held across the line.
   474	- **The mule** grazes across the stone with two feet either side and is not troubled.
   475	- **The floor (flag):** on the crown of a low swell, twenty-six of Cael's strides across by two diameters; colder than the turf; soundless under a boot; its rim rolled over like the rim of a dish, a hand above the turf outside and a finger above the floor within; level under a taut cord and under the brass and thread. Eleven squared grey uprights stand a stride in from the rim, each as thick as a man's body, the tallest twice Brom's height and the shortest knee-high. Round each, at chest height, and round the inner face of the lip, a band a hand wide of fine sunk lines, straight, hooked and crossing. None of Karis's forty-odd chips matches.
   476	- **The rubbings (flag):** one sheet to an upright, pencil rubbed over paper held flat by Lira; ten whole and the eleventh half done; numbered, with each stone's height and facing. They are in the middle box (the eleventh rolled inside Karis's coat).
   477	- **The den:** under the eastern lip, where the slope falls away and the earth has slumped from beneath the rim: a mouth as long as a cart and thigh-high, running back under the floor; a trodden apron; at least four worn hollows in a row; coarse hair on the underside of the lip.
   478	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the Oxhollow carter; the carter with the split hand; the crew with the wooden foot.
   479	
   480	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   481	**Calendar.** Sunset on the **ninety-sixth day past the Line**, at the eastern lip of the Fallow Ring's floor. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term"; "frost"; "before the snow"; "fortnight"). Days on the page: 84 (dusk), 85, 86, 87, 88, 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95, 96; and, in Teague's window, the evening of the 89th. Weather: frost every morning; dry roads; a north wind on the Fallow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
   482	
   483	**Where everyone is.** Six people at the den under the eastern lip, ten cords and half a floor inside the line: Cael, Lira, Brom (with the middle box on his back), Karis, Seln (on one knee) and Oryn. The lane trodden by the cord runs from the floor's western lip two hundred yards and more down to the grey stone. Oryn's mule is hobbled on the western side of the stone with her panniers, six of Thornwater's blankets, the carter's left boot and the long shape in its oiled cover still lashed on the pack. No hound has been seen. The sun is half under the hills.
   484	
   485	**Cael.** Eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly.** Logged deployment since the Line: one (the ford); the day-96 entry carries no deployment line. **On the 96th, unwitnessed:** the Iron read opened a pace short of the line and carried across it; a foot's worth of Wind evasion twenty strides inside; the Iron read once more on the floor. Shadow is sealed and untouched; it lifted at dusk on the 95th at the turn of the ruts and on the 96th inside the line, and was refused both times. Anchor asked at the stone and at the stake. Ribs: taped, eighteen days from the break, three to go. Calf: bound in Oryn's linen, closing by itself. He has told nobody that anything answered him inside, and has given Karis "It was dark before… It's dark now." for her sixth row. Hesk's satchel holds the log, the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter, Pike's lines on bark, and now Ephram's letter and Vell's. He has not answered any of the three.
   486	
   487	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst since the 78th; on the 96th she asked for a whole one inside the line and took one step of a yard and a half. Right tibia mended on the 80th: walking; not to run until about the 110th. **Right ankle not mended**, taped by herself; the carter's right boot over a torn shirt. Long knife drawn at the last line.
   488	
   489	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. Called the Skin aloud inside the line; none. Left wrist sound; the cuff came off at the middle of the 87th and is inside his shirt with Pike's dead slip. He has carried the middle box from Thornwater. He has the hedging bill.
   490	
   491	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Refilled: one point on a wick on the 93rd, "three parts full"; nothing since (roads). Inside she can see every place and set none. Holds: the perimeter table (sixty-two stations, one offset); the six-row table of the trials, with the sixth ruled on his statement; ten rubbings in the box and an eleventh, half done, in her coat; the carter's map with three pencilled rings, two marked *guess*; the registry's sheet; the long cord and the short one; the brass; forty-odd chips; the leaf with her nine crossings. She has said "A circle is a made shape." once, and nobody took it up.
   492	
   493	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Nothing to lift inside. Unmarked. He has read the posting as an office's paper, written by somebody who learned to write inside the Line; the stake as five years in the weather and driven at the far end of a measure; the eastern runs; the den. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.**
   494	
   495	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. At Thornwater on the 93rd; one mending there (the carter's hand) and two more that evening. She gave the five her days until the 99th of her own choice, for two reasons. She has taken a surface reading of Brom's wrist on the 94th and at the stone on the 96th; **inside the line she reads nothing and has read nobody.** She has crossed with Brom (twice together, once alone with his arm held across) and with the six. She knows nothing of fragments and has no theory. She cannot mend until she is west of the stone.
   496	
   497	**Teague.** At Lowmarch on the 91st with his three. He read the Fallow posting twice on the 89th and put it back; he has no reason and has given none. He watched the five take it. The two holds' slips are his if the five are not down inside the fortnight.
   498	
   499	**Objects and money.** The middle box: chips, lamp, paper, ten rubbings, the two maps, the brass, the short cord. Two boxes in Thornwater's loft, the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. The Oxhollow cart has gone home with grain. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and eight in hand.** Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman, with no price. A hundred marks lie in Pike's box against the broker's mark on the Fallow paper. Cael's coat has no sleeves.
   500	
   501	**Pike's book and the wall.** **Seven lines**; the seventh is the protected traverse line with *less six, the cart.* under it. **Tenth; Teague nine up.** The Fallow posting carries five strokes and is in Hesk's book. Two slips with HOLD and the five names are on the oak.
   502	
   503	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five, and nobody else. All six know what quiet ground does to a live Arbiter, to the stride, and that a healer's hands stop there. The party knows that the registry's iron stands on the line, numbered thirty-one, some five years old; that no instruments are at the site; that hounds den under the floor. **Nobody but Cael knows that anything answers inside.** Pike, Teague, the clerk and the holds know only that five people took a paper.
   504	
   505	**Open threads.** Where eight or nine hounds are, at sunset. A lane two hundred yards long and one way out that anybody has walked. A healer who cannot mend east of a stone. A captain's quarter of an hour. What he has not told them, and why. A posting written inside the Line for a thing that is not there. Thirty-one. Two blanks on a calfskin marked *guess*. "A circle is a made shape." A rope, two holds, three unanswered letters, and no roof.
   506	
   507	### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
   508	Movement 5 must inherit the following facts not fully carried by its packet:
   509	
   510	- **Correct Ring geometry:** western line stone to floor centre is 220 yards as printed; the floor is 26 of Cael's yard-strides across; the den is at the eastern lip. Therefore den to the western objective line is about 233 yards, and den to the eastern perimeter is about 207 yards. Do not reintroduce M4's discarded 150-yard figure (`manuscript/chapter-30.md:43-45,165-169,199-201`; `MOVEMENT-004.md:64-66`). The cord is approximately 21.9 yards by the protected circumference/radius figures, but its length and Karis's stride remain unstated (`manuscript/chapter-28.md:191-209`).
