     1	# Chapter 11 — The Quiet
     2	
     3	He counted the floor going back across it, because he had counted it coming, and it was twenty-six strides both ways.
     4	
     5	Positions, catalogued as they came off the ring with the light going: Seln first, over the floor and down the west lip into the scrub without a sound, gone toward the road. Karis after him with the book under her arm, the cord over her shoulder, charcoal to the wrists. Oryn, her leather roll on its strap across her back and her hands at her sides. Lira, on the taped ankle. Brom last but one, with the middle box on his back by its strap and the carter's crossbow across the top of it — Oryn's crossbow, which had ridden under her mule's panniers for two years because carters carried them through quiet ground, and which she had handed to Brom at the stone that afternoon without being asked, saying she had never once wound it at anything and did not mean to start; six bolts in a leather sleeve on his belt beside the hook. And himself, last, with nothing in his hands and nine things held to nothing, walking the last five strides backward.
     6	
     7	The floor had gone white along its whole width at once, as it had the evening before, and it stayed white while the grass round it went grey.
     8	
     9	They came up over the far lip onto the white as the six of them went down off the near one into the grey, and for the length of a breath they were the only things on the floor, and the floor showed them to him as a page shows ink. Low. Grey. Moving close to the stone, and no sound in any of them. He counted, because a count was what he had. Nine. He counted again with his heels in the grass below the lip, and it was nine, and then the white went out of the last of them and they were in the scrub either side of the floor, and he could not have said where any one of them was.
    10	
    11	"Nine," he said, at the size Seln said things.
    12	
    13	The stone was ten cords west of the floor's middle, along the straight line Karis had laid her cord down that afternoon; the road's rut ran a stone's throw north of that line and met it at the stone.
    14	
    15	Seln's voice came back out of the scrub ahead, flat, with no more weight in it than a count.
    16	
    17	"The road's cut."
    18	
    19	Then, from further off: "The grass. Where the cord went."
    20	
    21	There was a dog in the rut, forty meters out from the floor, between them and the stone, because there was always a dog on the road and it was never the one you were looking at. What he had found in the dark was the lane that was not cut — the cord-line, grass and low scrub, south of the rut and parallel to it, meeting the road again at the stone.
    22	
