books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:109:"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md-110-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md-111-"Yes," Cael said.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md-112-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md-113-"The route's eleven days north of here." She said it as she had said *Twelve, in two days* at Thornwater's gate: a count, not a complaint. "It was nine when I sat down at your fire at the bend, and it was a day I owed. Now it's a day and two nights and three mendings and a mule that's eaten what a mule eats in three days. Every day I sit here the route doesn't get shorter. It gets longer at the far end, where I can't see it."
--
