     1	# Chapter 31 — Nine on the White
     2	
     3	Seln stood up, and the floor went white.
     4	
     5	The sun had gone under the hills while Cael's book was shutting. He had seen the pale thing hold the light on the evening before, from three hundred yards, and had taken it for water; now he stood below its eastern lip, with the rolled edge at the height of his thigh, and saw what it did close to. The long shadows of the stones were gone, and the whole round of it lay level with his hip and gave back the sky, as milk in a pan gives back a window.
     6	
     7	He went over the ground once in his head, since six people were about to walk it with the light going.
     8	
     9	They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
    10	
    11	He had one other mark on that slope. North of the lane a rut came down from under the floor's north-western lip, knee-deep and a stride across, where the rain off the rim had cut itself a road. He had marked the head of it from the ninth cord, and Seln had crossed it on both his rounds in the grass.
    12	
    13	"Cael," said Lira.
    14	
    15	She had not raised her voice. She was looking between two of the uprights, with the knife held low.
    16	
    17	Something grey had come onto the white.
    18	
    19	It came over the western lip, where the lane ended, and it came without any sound at all, as everything went on that floor. It was low and long in the back, and it carried its head level, and he had seen that shape beside a white stone in a valley and on a bar of gravel with a river round it. A second came up behind the first, and a third; and then they were coming too quickly to be watched singly, and he counted them as he would have counted fighters onto a yard.
    20	
    21	The first was pale, nearly white along the spine, so that against that floor he could see little of it but the legs. The heaviest had a dark stripe from its neck to the root of its tail. Two smaller ones came over shoulder to shoulder and stayed so. There was one long in the leg, which moved like a thing built for running, and one grey to the eyes, which walked a little way onto the white and looked about it and lay down. Three more he could not have told from each other.
    22	
    23	"Nine," he said.
    24	
    25	"Nine," said Seln. "I am glad to have the figure. I could not get it from their feet."
    26	
    27	They did not hurry, and it was that which he liked least. They spread out among the eleven stones as a crew spreads out in its own yard at the end of a day. One sat and scratched. The pale one stood at the middle of the round, by the place where a cord had ended, with its nose down on the grey.
    28	
    29	They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings.
