     1	# Chapter 34 — Maps with Holes
     2	
     3	It was that same night, the ninety-sixth going into the ninety-seventh, and Seln had the south.
     4	
     5	He stood forty yards from the lamp, in grass to his hip, where its light did not reach him and his own eyes were not spoiled by it. He had the line on his left hand. He could not see it. He knew where it was by a strip of burnt stems that began ten yards from his boot, and by a thing behind his breastbone that had gone out at a stone on the way in, and had been there again at the same stone on the way out, exactly where he had left it.
     6	
     7	He had called a word up a hill tonight, at the top of his voice, and he could not remember when he had last raised it.
     8	
     9	The camp was quiet behind him. He listened to it as he listened to any room that he had his back to, and sorted what he heard: a pencil, and then no pencil; a big man washing his hands without a sound; and the healer's breathing, which was slow and went all the way down.
    10	
    11	"Seln. Say yours."
    12	
    13	The boy had asked it an hour ago, across the lamp.
    14	
    15	He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
    16	
    17	Tonight he had been asked, by name, for the first time. And the whole of what he had to hand in was one word.
    18	
    19	It was an accurate word. He had submitted a great many accurate reports, and few of them had sat so ill.
    20	
    21	He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
    22	
    23	It had been right. A bow at the lip had been worth more than a second knife, and he would have gone again. He noted only that it was the same price each time, and that somebody else always paid it. He had no column for that, and he found, a little to his surprise, that he would have liked one.
    24	
    25	There was the other thing, and he came to it when he had finished with himself, as he came to the difficult page of any file.
    26	
    27	He had been looking straight back up the lane. That was his place; the man on point looks behind him as often as before. He had seen the small one go down, and a grey animal step out between her and his knife, and beyond those the two women, and the box. Behind the box there had been a boy with a blade in his hand.
    28	
    29	Then there had been the lane, and the grass, and the box.
    30	
    31	Seln had not seen him go. Nobody sees that. It is the whole of the craft that there is no instant at which a person is seen to leave; the eye passes over the place and finds it not worth resting on, and goes to something with more in it. He had learned it on a back stair when he was younger than any of them. It had taken him a year to do on purpose, and three more to stop doing by accident whenever a door slammed.
    32	
    33	He had known it this time from the outside, by two signs. A big man's eye had slid over a piece of air and come back to it. And along the south edge of the lane the grass had leaned, stem by stem, with nothing leaning on it.
    34	
    35	It had gone on for ten strides. Then there had been a boy with both hands on a hound.
    36	
    37	Seln had done the sum himself, in the half-breath before. It was the first thing that a person of his trade did on entering any room, and he had done it without choosing to: who is here, and what can each of them see, and where will each of them be tomorrow. There were four who had known for years and had been tried. There was a healer, who was owed everything and knew nothing, and who walked seven roads. She had her back turned and a hundredweight of copied stone between.
    38	
    39	He would have said *go*. He had not needed to. The boy had stood there for as long as a jaw takes to shut, doing the same sum, and had got the same answer; and he had lifted it, and carried it exactly as far as it was wanted, and put it down with his hands already full.
    40	
    41	It was correct. Seln looked for a better word and did not find one.
    42	
    43	He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.
    44	
    45	He did not propose to say so to anyone.
    46	
    47	The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	The stars went over, and the frost came down, and toward the end of the watch he let himself think about the iron.
    52	
    53	He had been keeping off it since noon. It had stood at the back of everything that he did that day, as a figure stands at the back of a room: a square bar, driven at the far end of a measure, with a stamp on its neck that he had seen on every door he had walked through for fifteen years.
    54	
    55	Thirty-one.
    56	
    57	He had told the boy that he did not know what the number counted, and that he would not guess. Both of those were true. There was a third thing which he had not said, because it was not yet a thing: something in him had answered to that iron, and it was not the Shadow.
    58	
    59	He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
    60	
    61	The big man came down out of the north when the east had begun to grey, walking as a person walks who has stood since midnight, with a bow in two bundles of rag.
    62	
    63	"Yours is over," said Brom. "Nothing?"
    64	
    65	"Nothing."
    66	
    67	"Same. I'd sooner there'd been something. I'd know where it was."
    68	
    69	"That is very well put," said Seln, and meant it.
    70	
    71	He did not go to his blanket. He went along the line to the north, with the burnt strip on his right hand and then the unburnt grass, counting Brom's turfs as he came to them. They lay grass downward, each a cord from the last, and the frost had made them grey. At the third he stopped.
    72	
    73	It stood where it had stood. He crouched over it without touching it, as he had crouched at noon, and waited for the light.
    74	
    75	It came slowly. First there was a bar, and then the head of it, spread by a mallet. Then there was the stamp.
    76	
    77	He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
    78	
    79	A case with a strap.
    80	
    81	The drawer came open.
    82	
    83	It was a tin, as long as his forearm, with a cap at one end and a lead tag crimped through the hasp. He had it under his arm. He was young, in the memory, with a satchel and one key, and he was carrying the tin from one room to another; that was the whole of the errand. A junior was permitted to break the tag in the second room, before a senior, and to draw out what was in the tin and count it against a docket. He was to count. He was not to read.
    84	
    85	There had been eight sheets. They were fair copies, on paper backed with linen, and they were of the east.
    86	
    87	He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
    88	
    89	And on some of the sheets, out in the white, there had been a round.
    90	
    91	It was a small exact circle, ruled with a compass. He remembered the prick of the point at its middle, where the paper had been dented. There was nothing inside it at all; a dashed road came up to one and went about it and on. At the foot of each sheet there had been a little table of signs, a ford, a hold, a hill, and no sign among them for a round with nothing in it. On one sheet there were two.
    92	
    93	He had counted eight, and said *eight*, and the senior had initialled the docket; and a young man with a satchel had gone down a stair thinking about his dinner.
    94	
    95	He had taken those rounds for what a junior takes everything for, which is a thing that he has not been told. He had supposed a post, or a store, with a guard on it. It had never once entered his head that a place might be left off a map because of what the ground itself did there.
    96	
    97	Seln stayed on his heels by the iron a long while, and went over what he had, strictly, as he would have gone over it for somebody else's signature. He had eight sheets, seen once, by a boy who was counting. He had rounds, ruled, with a road going about one of them. He had a stake under his eyes, of the pattern that is signed for, driven on the edge of a circle that a researcher had paced with her feet.
    98	
    99	He did not have the leaf. There would be one. A sheet with a hole in it has a page somewhere that says what the hole is for; that was how every office that he had ever served drew breath. He had never seen it. He did not know what room it lay in, or under what heading, or whose hand had ruled those rounds. He knew the weight of a tin and the number of its sheets, and he knew that he would be asked for more than that before the sun was high, by a small person with a pencil and no voice.
   100	
   101	He could not tell, from memory, where on those sheets a round had fallen. He had the look of one: a water crossed, and dashes running on east of it, and then the white place. To say more than that he needed a ford drawn by somebody who had put a wheel through it.
   102	
   103	He stood up, and his knees cracked in the cold.
   104	
   105	The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
   106	
   107	He went down on one knee in front of her, so that she should not have to lift her chin.
   108	
   109	"I should like the carter's map," said Seln. "And the charcoal, if it came east in that box."
