   221	It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
   222	
   223	The steading's woman had given the healer her back room without being asked; and when Karis inquired whether that was the custom, she had looked at her as at a person asking whether the well had water in it.
   224	
   225	"It's hers when she's here," the woman said. "It's nobody's when she's not."
   226	
   227	There was a bench under its window, and by dusk there were seven people on it, three of the hold and the rest come in with the east carts, and one of them a child. Oryn came out when the shadow of the fence had crossed the well, and washed to the elbow, and went along them as he had seen her go along eight at Oxhollow; and she chose two.
   228	
   229	That was no longer new to him, and he did not count her lips; he watched the yard instead, where nobody stood about. He had seen a healing house's corridor once, from a bed, and it had been full of people waiting to be told their turn by tier. Here the five whom she had passed over got up from the bench without complaint and went back to what they had been doing, and the woman of the hold set a lamp on the back room's sill, unlit, and a bowl beside it.
   230	
   231	Oryn glanced at the lamp, and then across the yard.
   232	
   233	"You. Ember."
   234	
   235	Karis was on the well's coping with her notebook. She looked up.
   236	
   237	"I told you a week, on a mountain, and three days on top of it at a ford. That ran out on the eighty-seventh, and this is the ninety-third. Have you asked it for anything since?"
   238	
   239	"No."
   240	
   241	"Why not?"
   242	
   243	"Because I have been on roads, where one does not. And because—" Karis stopped. She did not often stop in the middle of a sentence. "Because I looked for the place, the first night, and it was gone. I had sooner not look again and find it gone a second time."
   244	
   245	"That's honest." Oryn jerked her head at the sill. "This is a yard, and I want a light. Come here."
   246	
   247	Karis got down, and crossed the yard with her hands held a little away from her sides, as she had carried them when they were bound, and stood before the lamp. Cael saw her draw a breath. Then her right hand made the smallest dip that he had ever seen it make, as a person sets a full stop.
   248	
   249	A seed of yellow stood on the wick.
   250	
