     1	# Chapter 29 — A Stride Inside
     2	
     3	They held the trials at the stone, at noon, and Karis set the order.
     4	
     5	"Smallest first," she said. She had ruled a fresh leaf into five rows and given it to Cael, with the pencil. "A one-armed man has told us that nothing can be declared in there, and five of us felt our Arbiters go, and I believe both. I believed in a ledge as well. There are prints in the grass on the far side of this field, and I am not going to learn that he was mistaken by having Lira crack the sky open a quarter of a mile from whatever left them."
     6	
     7	"I'd not crack it on purpose," said Lira.
     8	
     9	"You did not crack it on purpose the last time. That is rather my point." Karis looked round at them. "So I go first, with one point, unlit. If it sets, I shall know it, and nobody else will, and we stop there and think. If mine does not, then Brom. Then Seln. You are last of the four, and you go only if the three of us have had nothing."
    10	
    11	"And outside the line nobody tries anything," said Seln. "It is a road. I should like that kept."
    12	
    13	"Nobody tries anything outside. We know what we are outside."
    14	
    15	The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
    16	
    17	Cael looked at the leaf that he had been given. There were five rows on it, each headed with a letter, and his own was not among them. That was as it should be, since he had been handed the pencil for the very reason that there was nothing of his to enter. He had kept the count at a great many trials of other people, from the east tier of a yard, and had thought himself used to the seat.
    18	
    19	Karis stepped over.
    20	
    21	Cael saw her shoulders tighten as her foot came down, and ease again; she had done this nine times in the dark, she had told them at first light, and it plainly did not grow pleasanter. She stood a stride inside the line, with her back to the ring, and her eyes went slowly along the slope in front of her from left to right, as he had seen them go over a district floor before a bout.
    22	
    23	"I can see them," she said. "Every place. I could put sixteen down on that bank and tell you where each would sit, to the hand. That is not altered in the least."
    24	
    25	"That's only knowing how," said Brom.
    26	
    27	"It is knowing how, and it is still here; I want it written that it is still here." Her right hand had come up. "Now I am going to set the first, eleven paces off, by that tuft with the frost on it."
    28	
    29	The hand dipped, the small exact dip of a person dotting a page.
    30	
    31	Nothing happened that he could see, and that proved nothing, for a set point gave no light; so he watched her face instead, and it had the look of somebody who has put a foot down for a stair in the dark and found the floor.
    32	
    33	"No," said Karis. She tried it a second time, and a third, quickly. "No. It has not set. A lecturer would say that the declaration did not render. And that is not the whole of it. When I am empty and try to set one, I feel myself reach into the purse and close my fingers on nothing. Here I am not even reaching. I am three parts full, and I can't find the hand to reach with."
    34	
    35	"Does it hurt?" said Oryn, from behind the stone.
    36	
    37	"Not in the least."
    38	
    39	"Does it tire you? Trying."
    40	
    41	Karis thought about that with real care.
    42	
    43	"It costs nothing. There's nothing to spend."
    44	
    45	She stepped back over. He saw the colour come into her face as the foot came down, and her eyes go, for an instant, to something at the edge of her sight that nobody else could see.
    46	
    47	"Back," she said. "First row. Write it as I gave it: every place seen; none set; no cost."
    48	
    49	He wrote it, and looked at it a breath longer than a line needed. He had sat in a whitewashed room for the better part of a year while that small exact person laid points on a wall for him to study, and he carried one of them still, the fifth of his nine, with its rent paid; and he had not known until this noon that there was a place anywhere where her hand could make that dip and have nothing come of it.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	Brom went over with his sleeves rolled.
    54	
