    57	It was only then, with the first rush over, that he noticed what was under him.
    58	
    59	It was cold; he felt it through his soles, and it was a deeper cold than the turf's. It gave back no sound whatever. Lira had come across it in one boot of her own and one the size of a bucket, and Brom with a hundredweight on his back, and neither of them had rung. The low sun came over from the south-west and threw the shadows of eleven stones in long bars to the far side, and between the bars the grey shone a little, dully, as a slate shines that has been wetted.
    60	
    61	He paced it from west to east, past her notebook, and had twenty-six strides. He paced it again from south to north, and had twenty-six. By the time he came back with the second figure, nine twists of paper lay opened in a row along the lid of the middle box, each with its chip set back in it and a small cross pencilled on the paper.
    62	
    63	Karis and Brom had the long cord stretched across the whole width by then, to learn whether the thing was level; and it was the cord that found the edge for them.
    64	
    65	"She won't lie down," said Brom, from the western side. He had his end drawn to the ground outside, and the cord stood clear of the grey for a yard in from the brink. "There's a rise on it. Look here."
    66	
    67	Cael came and looked. All along the brink, as far as he could follow it to either hand, the floor turned up and rolled over on itself, smooth, with no corner anywhere, so that its crest stood a finger above the grey within and a hand above the turf outside. He had stepped over that a quarter of an hour ago and never marked it. A person running would have had to.
    68	
    69	"A lip," said Karis, and wrote the word.
    70	
    71	Something stirred in him at that rolled crest and asked its one question, as it asked of every arris and sill. Blade wished to know whether the thing was an edge; and for once Cael could not have told it.
    72	
    73	They held the cord a hand high after that, from crest to crest, and she went along beneath it on her knees with the short one.
    74	
    75	"Level," she said, at the far side. "The same gap the whole way over, to the thickness of a knot." She had the brass straightedge, with its pebble on a thread, and she laid it toward each quarter of the floor in turn. "And no pitch. I laid this brass on a mountain for seven days, and on every hundred of it the thread hung off its mark by so many hands. Here it hangs true whichever way I point it. A threshing floor is not as flat as this. A table is not."
    76	
    77	"What is it, though?" Brom had let the box down beside her nine crossed twists, and he crouched with one big hand spread on the grey. "It's not slab; slab's got a grain. It's not laid, either. I can't find a course."
    78	
    79	"I am most of the way to telling you what it is not."
    80	
    81	She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
    82	
    83	"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
    84	
    85	Cael had knelt down a little way off while she sorted.
    86	
    87	He put both palms on the floor. It was smooth, and very slightly rough under the skin, like the shell of an egg. He moved his hands outward over it as far as his ribs would let him, feeling for the thing that every floor he had ever knelt on possessed somewhere: a crack, or a line where one piece met the next.
    88	
    89	His fingers found none. He knew that fingers could be wrong on a surface so close, and that the others were all looking at three small stones in a researcher's lap.
    90	
    91	He opened the read.
    92	
    93	It was the same thing that he had done at the line, done now for work, and he did not count it a third. For a pace and a half round his knees, and a pace and a half straight down, he had the weight of what he was kneeling on, and it was one thing. There was no bed under it, and no joint through it, and no place where it changed. The short upright stood within his reach, and he took in the foot of that as well. It went down into the floor as a horn goes into a skull, and he could find no line between them.
    94	
    95	He closed it again. Nothing had come through the grass.
    96	
    97	He had known since a hill of plates what he thought of a reading that nobody else could check, and it had not altered. So he took the pencil and wrote in Karis's table, under her *level*, the only part of what he knew that any one of them could have got with a hand and a pair of eyes.
    98	
    99	*No seam.*
   100	
   101	"Nor at the foot of the stones?" said Karis. She had read it upside down.
   102	
   103	"Not that I can find."
   104	
   105	"Then I shall write that two of us looked." She put it down. "It is a poor sort of catalogue. *Round. Level. No seam. Matches nothing.* I have written four things about it and three of them are that something is absent."
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	She began on the bands when the sun was a little past its height, and it took the rest of the afternoon. She would not copy by eye, since she said that an eye puts in what it expects to see. She laid a clean sheet against a band, and had Lira hold it flat with both hands, and rubbed the side of her pencil over it until the lines came up white out of the grey.
   110	
   111	She took a sheet to a stone. She numbered each in its corner, and wrote beside the number how high the stone stood and which way it faced; and Brom carried them to the box one at a time, on the flat of his two hands, as he would have carried a tray of eggs.
   112	
   113	She had come to the ninth stone when Seln spoke.
   114	
   115	"There is nothing here," he said.
   116	
   117	He had let them work. Cael had seen him go about the floor, in no hurry, with his hands behind him and his eyes down; he had been to the foot of each of the eleven stones, and all round the outside of the lip, and twice he had gone off into the grass and come back. Now he stood at the middle, by the cord's end.
   118	
   119	"I have been looking for what we were sent to fetch. A case, I supposed, or a tripod, or a chain. A peg. The mark where a peg had been." He turned one hand over. "There is nothing on this floor but ourselves, and nothing at the foot of any stone. I have walked a round in the grass at twenty yards from the lip, and another at forty. I find our own track, coming in from the west. I find nothing that a person has set down, or dropped, or driven."
   120	
   121	"It's been five years," said Brom. "By your stake."
   122	
   123	"It has. Leather would be gone, and wood. Brass would not, nor iron. And I should expect to see where a thing had lain, on turf that has never been grazed." He looked at Cael. "I do not say that nothing was ever left. I say that I cannot find it, and that I have looked as well as I know how."
   124	
   125	Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
   126	
   127	There were no instruments. There was a stake on the line with a registry's stamp on it, and that was the only thing within a quarter of a mile that a surveyor had ever touched.
   128	
   129	"He said he'd write what was done," said Lira slowly. She had her two hands spread on a sheet of paper against the ninth stone. "The old man. If the clerk's mark never goes on it, the box stays shut."
   130	
   131	"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
   132	
   133	"For eleven sheets and a line," said Karis, without lifting her pencil. "I should not undersell the sheets."
   134	
   135	Cael looked at the sun.
   136	
   137	It had come round into the west while they worked, and it stood now above the hills that they had crossed two days ago. He put his arm out straight and laid his hand along the skyline, fingers together, and then his other hand on top of that. The lower edge of the sun sat on his upper knuckle.
   138	
   139	Two hands. He had said it at the eastern runs that morning, with a print in front of him, and Seln had said that he would hold him to it.
   140	
   141	"Two stones more," said Karis. She had seen what he was doing. "The tenth and the eleventh. A quarter of an hour, if Lira doesn't sneeze. I shall never stand here again; you heard what he said about going in twice."
   142	
   143	He weighed it. He had set a limit, and he knew what a limit was worth that gave by a quarter of an hour the first time it was leaned on. He also knew that nine sheets out of eleven was a thing she would carry for the rest of her life. And the hillside was as empty as it had been at noon. No bird had got up. Nothing had stirred the grass.
   144	
   145	"The two," he said. "No more. Seln, go round the far side once, by the east, and see that we've missed nothing under the lip. When you're back, we're in that lane and walking."
   146	
   147	"You said two hands," said Seln mildly.
   148	
   149	"I did. I'm spending a quarter of an hour that I told you I wouldn't, and I want it entered against me."
   150	
   151	Seln gave the small bow that he gave to an order he disliked, and went.
   152	
   153	Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
   154	
   155	"It's a queer thing," she said, to nobody. "I've been on a great many floors with people on them. This is the first where I couldn't have told you which of you was tired."
   156	
   157	"I feel thirteen," said Lira.
   158	
   159	She had said it lightly, with her hands on the tenth sheet. Then she seemed to hear it herself.
   160	
   161	"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	Karis finished the eleventh sheet at the quarter-hour.
   166	
   167	She numbered the last corner and rolled the sheet, and for once she did not stop to look at what she had got. Brom had set the box at the foot of the tallest stone, on the eastern side, an hour ago, to keep ten sheets of paper out of the north wind. He knelt there now with his back to it and reached behind him for the strap, and Cael went to steady the weight onto his shoulders, since that was a thing that could be done with one arm.
   168	
   169	"There's no ground under this bit," said Brom.
   170	
   171	He was looking down past his own knee. The box stood a yard from the lip; and beyond the lip, on that side, the turf that came up to the floor's edge everywhere else was not there.
   172	
   173	Cael leaned out over the rolled crest. The slope fell away to the east more steeply here, and at some time the earth beneath the rim had slumped and been carried off, so that the grey stone stood out over nothing, like the eave of a roof. Thigh-deep below it there was bare ground, trodden as hard as a threshing floor.
   174	
   175	There was a print in it, directly under his face. It was as broad as a palm, without a claw, and its edge was sharp where it had broken the morning's frost.
   176	
   177	He had time to know what it was. Then something shifted, four yards along the foot of the overhang, and it was Seln. He was down on one knee at the border of the trodden earth, where he must have been for some minutes, with one hand lifted a little from his side.
   178	
   179	"I had been about to call you," he said, very low, without turning his head. "I find that I do not care to raise my voice in this place. Bring the others round by the turf, and bring them quietly."
   180	
   181	They came, all of them, and stood where the ground still was.
   182	
   183	Under the lip there was a hollow. It ran back beneath the floor farther than Cael could see, low and dark, with a mouth as long as a cart and as high as his thigh.
   184	
