   190	
   191	Nothing was in them. He checked that twice. The hollow was empty, as far as light went, and no sound came out of it, and the wind brought him only the smell of cold earth and something rank and dry.
   192	
   193	Seln had not risen, and he had touched nothing. He was reading, with his head a little on one side, as Cael had seen him read a wet margin beside a pool on a mountain.
   194	
   195	"This morning," he said, very low. "Before we had laid the first cord. More than six. I should say eight, or nine; they have gone over each other's marks and I cannot do better." He pointed, without lifting his hand from his knee, at the newest prints. They led away from the mouth, down the slope, to the east. "Out. All of them, and none back since."
   196	
   197	"Then where are they?" said Lira. It was hardly a breath.
   198	
   199	"I do not know. I have seen no animal on this hillside since the sun rose, and I have been looking for nothing else."
   200	
   201	Brom had come up on Cael's other side, with the box on his back. He was looking at the bones.
   202	
   203	"Why here?" he said. "There's a thousand hollows in a country like this. There's a whole mountain two days west with caves in it. Why would a thing with teeth choose to lie under the one floor that the carters won't walk on?"
   204	
   205	Seln did not turn his head.
   206	
   207	"They live in here because nothing that walks in can fight."
   208	
   209	It lay on the cold air. Cael heard it, and so did the rest, and he watched it reach each of them in turn.
   210	
   211	A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
   212	
   213	Lira was looking west.
   214	
   215	They all were. He saw their heads turn, one after another, across twenty-six strides of grey floor and the eleven stones, to the lane that the cord had left in the grass. It ran away from them down the slope, straight, pale in the low light. The grey stone at its end was too small to see. He knew where it was. From the middle of the floor it lay two hundred and twenty yards to the west, and they were standing on the wrong side of the floor from it.
   216	
   217	Then they looked at the sun.
   218	
   219	It was on the ridge. He had measured two hands under it and spent the quarter of an hour he had given away; now the lower rim of it was touching the hills, red and flattened, and going. The shadows of the eleven stones had run out across the floor and joined. Between them, where the last light struck it level, the grey had begun to shine.
   220	
   221	Then four of them looked at Oryn.
   222	
   223	She stood a little apart, with her broad hands hanging open. She had told them at noon what they had in her, on this side of a stone, and she met their eyes one after another and did not pretend that it had altered.
   224	
   225	And then, all five, they looked at him.
   226	
   227	He had the ruling. He was the nearest thing that they had to a captain, and it was his to say which way and how fast. That was the whole of what was in those five faces. Not one of them was asking him anything else.
   228	
   229	He stood with it. *Nothing that walks in can fight.* It was true of five of the six. He had known since a boulder that it was not true of the sixth, and he had told nobody, and he was the person they were all waiting on.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	He wrote it there, at the far lip, standing, with the book against his forearm.
   234	
   235	It took him less time than it took Brom to shift the box higher on his shoulders and draw the strap tight. He had always written at the start of a thing. He did not know how to begin one without it, and he thought that this might be the last quiet minute of the day.
   236	
   237	*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
   238	
   239	*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
   240	
   241	He looked at what he had put.
   242	
   243	There was a line that came next. He had written it at the foot of every entry since a shelf above a valley with no name, and for the last forty-three days it had ended in the same word. His pencil went to the place.
   244	
   245	He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
   246	
   247	Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
   248	
   249	He told it no.
   250	
   251	The sun was half gone. Along the foot of the eleven stones the floor had turned the colour of milk. Lira had drawn her knife, and Karis had the last sheet rolled inside her coat, and the healer was lacing her boot with quick hard pulls.
   252	
   253	Seln had not yet got up off his knee.

