     1	# Chapter 37 — Minute Twenty-One
     2	
     3	He counted breaths, since it was the only count that she had left him.
     4	
     5	A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
     6	
     7	For the first dozen there were only her hands. They were warm, and broad, and they did not move. He could feel the short nails of the left one against the back of his skull, and the heel of the right resting on bone. He could feel her pulse in it, which was slow.
     8	
     9	"That's skin," said Oryn.
    10	
    11	Her voice was lower than he had ever heard it, and it came from a little way off, as a person's does who is talking in her sleep.
    12	
    13	"That's breath. That's a heart, and it's a good one; somebody's run it hard since it was small. Those are the three I taped, and they're done. I said that they were."
    14	
    15	She was silent while he drew six more.
    16	
    17	"Past that, now."
    18	
    19	He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
    20	
    21	It did not go down. It went along.
    22	
    23	He felt it leave her right hand at about the thirtieth breath. It was not warmth, and it was not a touch; it was nearer to what a person feels when somebody else's attention comes to rest on the back of his neck. It set out from his breastbone toward his feet. It went the whole length of him, without haste, as water goes when a sluice is lifted at the head of a dry channel; and when it had reached as far as there was to reach, it turned and came back up the other side, and he felt it arrive under the nape of his neck, in her left hand, where she was waiting for it.
    24	
    25	Then it set out again.
    26	
    27	He lay very still. There was a beat in it. It was not his heart, which went a good deal faster, and it was not his breath. It was slower than either and it did not vary, and he knew it before he had found the word for where he knew it from. He had walked beside it for six days down a valley, at a mule's shoulder, with five bands of linen teaching him where his own edge was. It was the pace that she kept on a road. She was going through him at it.
    28	
    29	He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait. An assessor's panel had read him across a floor and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This had a gait. It favoured the right side by a hair, as she did, over a raw heel.
    30	
    31	He had been afraid, coming over the yard, that it would be like being searched. He had been searched by a good many careful people; he knew the hands of a clerk going through a satchel, and the eyes of a panel going over a stance for the thing that was wrong with it. This did not rummage. It went down him and came back as a carter goes down his own road, looking at what was there, and it took up nothing to see what lay beneath.
    32	
    33	"There," said Oryn.
    34	
    35	It was at the fifty-fourth breath.
    36	
    37	"There it is. I've got the moving. I had that much at a ford with two fingers." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "Now I go and look for what it's moving in."
    38	
    39	He drew the sixtieth, slowly, like a man asleep. That was five minutes, and she had only just come in.
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	She talked, as she had told him that she would.
    44	
    45	He had supposed that it would be like the talk at a bench, short and to the purpose. It was nothing of the kind. It was the low running murmur of a person who is feeling her way down a passage in the dark and saying what her hands meet, less to be heard than to keep herself from being lost; and he lay under it and counted, and tried to do as he had been told, which was nothing.
    46	
    47	It was a good deal harder than refusing.
    48	
    49	He had refused the nine at every stride of a road, and he knew that work as he knew the weight of his own boots. He was not to refuse them now; a refusal was a holding, and she had said that she would know. He was not to let them answer either. He had to lie as a house lies when somebody goes through it with a lamp, with every door standing as it happened to stand, and he found that he had never in his life left a door alone.
    50	
    51	"I'm looking for the sides of it," said Oryn. "The banks. Everybody's got them. They're the first thing I get, as a rule, before I've so much as got the pulse."
    52	
    53	Her current went down the length of him and came back.
    54	
    55	"Your big one's are like a wall. Both sides, straight up, as if somebody had built them. I could have leaned on them. That's a Skin, and I'd know one through a blanket."
    56	
    57	*Eighty-four.* It was passing his feet when she said it, and something in his feet turned over like a sleeper who has heard his name. It was Wind. He did not tell it no, and he did not let it rise. He let it turn over. It settled.
    58	
    59	"The small one runs hot. I felt the heat of hers before I'd found her wrist, on that mountain, and she was empty as a dry well at the time. That's an Ember. The bed's narrow and it's warm to the hand."
    60	
    61	His right palm had grown warm on the plank. He left it where it lay.
    62	
    63	"And the girl's goes by so fast that there's nothing to feel but the draught of it. You put your hand in and it's gone past already. That's what a Wind is like, under the hands. I had hold of hers for an hour at that stone and I'd not swear that I ever touched the bottom."
    64	
    65	She was silent for several breaths. He felt the thing that she was doing go more slowly along his left side, where three ribs had been, and then down the leg that a hound had opened, and stop there, and go on.
    66	
    67	[[TIER-B: Oryn during the long reading, her commentary on finding no banks in him, "there's more water" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
    68	
    69	He was at a hundred and eight. That was nine minutes.
    70	
    71	He had begun to be able to tell where she was. It was like lying in a field with his eyes shut while somebody walked round him: he had her by a sense that he had no name for, now at his knee, now under his heart. And as she came to each place, the thing that he carried there did what it would have done if a stranger had come to its door.
    72	
    73	Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
    74	
    75	"You're helping," said Oryn.
    76	
    77	His eyes came open. She had not opened hers.
    78	
    79	"Your neck went hard. Let it go. I don't need you to keep anything out of my way. I need you to lie there."
    80	
    81	He let it go. It took him three breaths.
    82	
    83	"That's it. That's a man asleep."
    84	
    85	She went on down.
    86	
    87	It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
    88	
    89	Her current went over the place and did not check.
    90	
    91	He breathed out. He had not known, until then, that he was afraid of what she might say.
    92	
    93	The heaviest of them she passed last, low down, under everything. He had felt it shift in a loft the night before, and it had been restless since a ditch; it had three lanes still in it, like a sea with a swell left over from weather a long way off. He waited for it to rise at her.
    94	
    95	It did not. It lay quiet under her hands. She went along the top of it as a boat goes over deep water, and it did not so much as lift.
    96	
    97	*A hundred and forty-four*, he counted. Twelve minutes. He lay with his neck loose and all nine doors standing as they stood, and for the first time since a morning in a windowless room there was somebody in the house besides himself.
