editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:*Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:*You were told. I shall fall more carefully.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:313:She took the first band at his breastbone, where its end was, and drew it off round his side to the spine in one pull, as she had laid it on. It came with a sound like a page tearing. The other four followed. He had expected to feel something give, and he felt only cold air on a place that had not had any for eighteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:101:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:"It can't get its legs under it. Keep close to the edge. If you give it a yard of ground it'll have its spring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:145:They were at the south. Fifteen yards on along the kerb two grey shapes were standing on the grass, shoulder to shoulder, with their heads level. They had gone ahead on the white and dropped off the rim where there was nobody under it with a pole, and they stood now on turf, where a hound has all the spring that it was born with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:307:"Up. That stays above her heart." She took Lira's left wrist and laid it against Lira's own right collarbone, and shut Lira's right hand over it. "You hold that there. You don't let it down to balance, and you don't put it out if you fall. If you fall, you fall on your face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:183:"I have something to show," he said. "I should like it shown where the iron is. It is three cords, and she may take my arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:207:Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:243:"The Compact knows what quiet ground does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:127:The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:285:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:299:He saw them only as shapes against the fire, and only for as long as it burned. They had been strung out down two hundred yards of hill behind a light, running to a declaration, and the declaration had gone over a stone and gone out; for he had let the flame die and the edge fall silent when the string slapped, and had shut his hand on all nine. What stood in front of them now was a fence of fire with their own road under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:11:"She runs the long reading at first light," said Cael. "On me, in the back room, with the door shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:"I've nine, and you know how each of them came. There was somebody with a Path, who used it on me or against me for long enough, and meant it; and there was something that mattered to both of us in how it came out, whether that was a card, or a ruling, or only which of us was left standing. That's the whole of what any of them had in common, and Karis has a page on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:It was a single leaf, and she had been two years in the making of it. It had a curve on it, for how quickly each of the nine had come, and a column for what each had cost him afterward; and at its head, in her small exact hand, stood three words with a rule under every one. She had never in his hearing called it more than a model. He had its three words by heart, and so had the others, since she had read them out on the night that the eighth was priced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:47:They read it. She was already writing again, and the second leaf came out after the first and was laid beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:59:"She doesn't know, and I don't, since there's been nothing to try it on. Every one of the nine came out of a fight of some kind. There's never been a person who did a real thing to me for a long time, at a cost, who wasn't standing on the other side of a floor." He touched the second leaf. "If she's right, tomorrow is the first morning of my life on which it could happen with nobody struck. And if it does, I shall have a piece of what a healer reads with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:"I've told her nothing. She has put her hands on me four times and found no banks, and she watched me go up a hill with fire in my fist, and in all that time she has never asked me one question that wasn't about a hurt. She thinks that she is going to lay me on a table and read me. If something is taken from her while she does it, she'll not have been told that it might be. She can't say yes to it, and she can't say no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:81:"The first is that if I tell her, it's a different morning. She would be reading a patient that she'd been warned about. She might keep some of herself back, to see; or she might go in looking for the very thing I'd named. I don't believe that either of those is the whole of her, and the page says that it has to be real. I'd have turned it into a trial."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:91:"I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner be known to that woman than be used to keep her ignorant of a thing that is to be done to her. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"I said something," said Lira. "In a lane, when I'd never heard of it either. You told me on the day you knew, and I didn't run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:151:Then she opened her mouth, and he saw what it cost her. The word came out cracked across the middle, and it was the third that she had spoken since a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:175:"She asked to read you," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:*Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:219:The flame had gone down to a blue bead, and he read the leaf through once by it, quickly, from the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:221:It was not a good page. He had hoped a little that it would look better written; that somewhere between the pencil and the paper it would turn into the kind of thing that a careful person does. It had not. It read as what it was: a boy wanted something, and had found that he could not have it honestly, and was going to take it and pay afterward; and he had set down the price first, in a hand that would be dry by morning, so that he could never claim to have been surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:That was all that the last line meant. A registry had read him for years, and written down what it found, and had never once told him beforehand what it was reaching for or what the reaching might cost him. He was about to reach for somebody else in the same way. The one thing that he had which that office had not was a leaf with tonight's date at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:247:Nine things lay in him, and he went along them as he went along anything at the start. Wind was in his feet, and still. Pressure sat under the breastbone. The read was shut, as it had been since a rut. Compression lay in his frame, and Ember in his right hand, with nothing to look at. The bright quick one had asked about the table's edge when he lay down, and Anchor about the sill, and he had told each of them no. The heaviest lay low, like weather that has blown itself out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:27:Brom did the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Throat and fingers mended at noon; nothing came. My calf read and not mended: the fourth time she's found nothing. The dead one drawn off south. The bolt cut out: five in the sleeve. Bread in sixths, by Brom, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:She let herself have that, for the length of a few breaths, since nobody else was going to give it to her. Twenty yards on a thing as round as a rail, in a boot like a bucket, on a leg that she had been forbidden to run on, with two dogs under her heels and one beside her knee; and she had been pulling away from all three. There had been nothing in it but legs. She had felt thirteen. She had felt the lanes at Fenmark come up under her as if they had only been waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:She knew what that was. She had read it in his book before she ever saw it, in a boy's cramped writing, with *sealed* beside it and a mild man's name left out. She had seen it since, a handful of times, and never once in front of anybody who did not already know. It had gone by her elbow like a draught, and then he was on the dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:Then he had set his feet. She had watched him set them, as she had taught him to in a lane in Ardenmere when they were both too young to know what he was; and the air had cracked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:103:She had not got that from anything he said. She had it from his face, a foot from her own, over a dog's head. There had been a thing in it that she had not seen before in all the years that she had read him across a floor. He had been *choosing*. A person does not choose against a thing that he does not know he can do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:She led the animal up until its forefeet were against the stone, and laid her whole hand on the warm neck under the mane, and stood with her head bent, reading whatever it is that a healer reads in a beast. Then, keeping her hand where it was, she stepped over, so that she stood inside with her arm stretched back across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:183:Karis was in front of her. Karis had not said a word since the lane, and could not, and she had her notebook in one hand and her eyes on a thing that she was working out; she walked as she always walked when she was thinking, without the least idea where the ground was. There was a place where a grain cart's wheel had cut the rut a hand deeper than the rest. She put her left foot into it and went over sideways like a dropped rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:193:It was too slow, and it was too clumsy. A right arm is not where a left arm would have been. Karis struck it and slid, and went down on one knee in the rut with a jolt that brought a noise out of her throat, a croak, the first sound that she had made since the lane; and the notebook flew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:247:She opened her mouth then, and Cael saw her throat work. It was the first time that she had tried since a lane. What came out was a small rough noise with the shape of a word in it, like a hinge that wants oil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:249:"No," said Oryn, without looking up from her heel. "Tomorrow, if it wants to. Not because you've a joke ready."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:He looked round the fire at five faces. Every one of them had watched a thing go north out of a lane that they had been certain could not happen, and had walked beside him for a day without a word about it. They had not gone quiet, or careful. The big man had let another person make his bread sooner than leave the others without, and the small one had made a joke of it on paper, and Lira had told a carter's boot what she thought of it. It was the same crew that had sat at a fire above a wash, with a healer added, who was looking at her own heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:261:He did not have one. He ate his bread, and it was very good, and he said so; and Brom told him that there was no call to be unkind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:337:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:351:*You get told.* He had four people waiting in a loft, and he knew already what he was going to lay in front of them. Those three words would be in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:29:He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait. An assessor's panel had read him across a floor and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This had a gait. It favoured the right side by a hair, as she did, over a raw heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:"And the girl's goes by so fast that there's nothing to feel but the draught of it. You put your hand in and it's gone past already. That's what a Wind is like, under the hands. I had hold of hers for an hour at that stone and I'd not swear that I ever touched the bottom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:67:[[TIER-B: Oryn during the long reading, her commentary on finding no banks in him, "there's more water" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:87:It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:93:The heaviest of them she passed last, low down, under everything. He had felt it shift in a loft the night before, and it had been restless since a ditch; it had three lanes still in it, like a sea with a swell left over from weather a long way off. He waited for it to rise at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:101:It was in the thirteenth minute that he understood what was strange about her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:111:He turned that over, slowly, at a man asleep's pace. She had watched him go up a hill with fire in his fist, in a place where she could not count to four, and she had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm. She had read his leg afterward and found nothing, and said so, and bound it in cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:It was not courage. He did not think that it had occurred to her that courage was wanted. It was her trade. A healer who stood amazed at a broken leg would never set it; she had spent her working life walking up to the worst thing in a yard and asking which it was, and her craft had no room in it for being impressed. He was the strangest thing that she had ever touched. To her that made him a difficult reading, and she was doing it as well as she could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:119:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was not weaker. It had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge. She looked for it under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:131:"I can feel that there's movement. More than one. I can't count them — I can't find where one stops. There's no channel. Everyone has a channel; I feel the banks and I know the Path. I can't find the banks in you. I've looked for twenty minutes. It isn't that you're broken. It's that I can't find anything shaped like the thing I read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:*More than one.* She had it. She did not know what she had. She had gone through a house with a lamp and counted more rooms than a house ought to hold, and she had stood in every one of them and could not find a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:And along the slow current that she kept, the one that set out from her right hand and came home to her left, he could feel a shape. It was not his. It was the way she went: the keeping of time, and the feeling ahead for a bank, and the patience of it, which would walk the length of a person eight times sooner than guess. It was the thing that she read with. She had laid the whole of it open on him for twenty minutes, in earnest, at a cost, and she was pressing it against him now harder than she had pressed it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:180:FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:181:[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:183:Note: current-perception component; architecture-reading component. Self and contact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:185:Acquisition: directed. Engagement: clinical — non-hostile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:190:He read it through, from the top, with her hand still on him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:192:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:222:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:230:He could have let her think so. He saw it open in front of him, as easy as a gate: it would have been a healer's hands doing something that they had never done before, to a boy that they could not read, and she would have carried it north with her and turned it over at every hold for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He had one other mark on that slope. North of the lane a rut came down from under the floor's north-western lip, knee-deep and a stride across, where the rain off the rim had cut itself a road. He had marked the head of it from the ninth cord, and Seln had crossed it on both his rounds in the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:19:It came over the western lip, where the lane ended, and it came without any sound at all, as everything went on that floor. It was low and long in the back, and it carried its head level, and he had seen that shape beside a white stone in a valley and on a bar of gravel with a river round it. A second came up behind the first, and a third; and then they were coming too quickly to be watched singly, and he counted them as he would have counted fighters onto a yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:They did not hurry, and it was that which he liked least. They spread out among the eleven stones as a crew spreads out in its own yard at the end of a day. One sat and scratched. The pale one stood at the middle of the round, by the place where a cord had ended, with its nose down on the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:It was Seln, and he said it as he would have read out a line from a bill of lading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:39:Brom looked at the lane then, and at what stood between themselves and the head of it, and said nothing more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:Behind him the slope fell away to the east, and the line on that side was nearer by twenty-six yards. It was also the hounds' own door. There were three runs across it and not a stride of it that any of the six had walked; the grass stood hip-high the whole way down, the ground was steep, and in a quarter of an hour it would be dark. If they came out alive on that side, they would be half a circle of open country from a mule. The west was a lane trodden flat by their own feet, with a stone at the end of it that five of them could find with their eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:55:"I shall go south about, at forty yards from the lip," said Seln. "I have walked that round twice today. Then down outside the lane, and up it with the thing in my hand. I should not be looked for before three hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:63:*One*, thought Cael. He priced it while he counted, since it was done. It took one of two knives off the hill, and the only one of the six who had nothing wrong with him, and against that it put a bow at the head of the lane. There was a third figure in the sum, which he looked at and did not say: something in his own feet could have had that bow on the floor in forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:Cael opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:183:Lira had run a quarter of the round. He counted it afterward at twenty yards. She was two strides short of the place where the lane came up to the lip, and she had three hounds strung out after her and nothing whatever in front, when the great boot came down on the outer curve of the roll and not on its top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:Everything that he carried had come up, as he had not felt it come since a mountain. Compression showed him the skull under his palms and how little it would take. Wind wanted his feet, and hers. And Ember had the place already, as it had had it once in a ford: he was looking into a yellow eye from a foot away, and it would set a point on anything that he looked at for long. *There.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:He could have ended it in a breath, three ways, and he went through what came after as quickly as he would have gone through a bout. Whichever he chose would go out of him on that floor a great deal louder than a healer's count, and eight heads would come round. There were two hounds within a yard of Brom's legs already, and Karis and the healer stood at the lip with nothing in their hands. He could take one animal, perhaps two; he could not take eight off four people who were standing inside the length of a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:245:Seln came up the lane at two hundred and seventy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"Thirteen feet," said Karis. Her voice was a thread. She could no more have helped it than he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:271:Cael did not move his hands. He had made the thing as still as he knew how to make it, and that was the whole of what he had to give. Nine fragments lay under his refusal, and not one of them could do what was wanted, which was to let a friend try. He looked across thirteen feet at one grey eye over a bow-stock, narrowed, and at a face that would have done anything on earth sooner than loose that string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:289:They got her over the lip to the head of the lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:293:[[TIER-B: Oryn at the rim, hands on Lira's bitten arm, "Nothing… Nothing in my hands." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:299:There was a good deal of blood, and it was black in that light. The teeth had met through the arm; he knew what that was, from the inside of his own calf, and he knew that his calf had been the smaller matter. He had seen her hurt in a dozen ways since a lane in Ardenmere, and he had never before seen so much of her on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:313:Then she looked at Cael. In that light her face was all planes, and there was a thing in it that he had not seen there at noon, when a count stopped in her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:319:The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:27:He had been looking straight back up the lane. That was his place; the man on point looks behind him as often as before. He had seen the small one go down, and a grey animal step out between her and his knife, and beyond those the two women, and the box. Behind the box there had been a boy with a blade in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:Then there had been the lane, and the grass, and the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had known it this time from the outside, by two signs. A big man's eye had slid over a piece of air and come back to it. And along the south edge of the lane the grass had leaned, stem by stem, with nothing leaning on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:39:He would have said *go*. He had not needed to. The boy had stood there for as long as a jaw takes to shut, doing the same sum, and had got the same answer; and he had lifted it, and carried it exactly as far as it was wanted, and put it down with his hands already full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:75:It came slowly. First there was a bar, and then the head of it, spread by a mallet. Then there was the stamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:83:It was a tin, as long as his forearm, with a cap at one end and a lead tag crimped through the hasp. He had it under his arm. He was young, in the memory, with a satchel and one key, and he was carrying the tin from one room to another; that was the whole of the errand. A junior was permitted to break the tag in the second room, before a senior, and to draw out what was in the tin and count it against a docket. He was to count. He was not to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:133:Brom was beside her already, on his knees, with the rags unwound. The cuts had dried black across eight finger-pads, and the two deep ones stood a little open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:[[TIER-B: Oryn mending Brom's string-cut fingers at noon on the 97th, "String… That's two." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:157:He counted with her, as he had counted on a mountain, and on a scrubbed table, twice. *One. Two.* He knew now what he had not known on any of those days. He had stood on a hill in the dark with every one of the nine going out of him at once, and she had been standing on a lane below him with her eyes open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:165:"I can't mend what I can't read. That's the rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:169:"I've seen him run up a hill. That's not the same as reading him." Oryn took her hands away and wiped them on her breeches. "And I don't say a finding four times to a patient and change it on the fifth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:191:[[TIER-B: Oryn sitting against the stake on the 97th, summing her mendings, "Three in two days… That's a route." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:209:"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:245:It lay on the afternoon like the iron itself. Cael looked at the stamp, a thumb's breadth below the beaten head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:267:Cael read them twice. They were the whole of it, and there was not a verb in them that reached past what six people had seen with their eyes. A map had a blank where this place was. A bar of iron had a stamp. And somewhere there was a page, which none of them would ever read, in a room that the one person who might have known could not name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:281:"A person who writes a sum twice, so that it cannot be altered, does not forget where he has put a brass level." Seln held out his hand for the sheet, and when he had it he held it up unread to the afternoon, as he had held it to a lamp, with the light behind. "I said in that inn that it had been written by somebody who learned to write inside. I said that it was not a reason against going. I should have gone on, and asked what it was a reason for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:287:Cael saw it then, as he saw a seam in a bout when it was already past mending. A paper had asked whether anybody who went into that ring would come out of it. He had carried the answer down a hill himself, with a light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:3:There were two hundred and seven yards of lane and eight hounds, and he had both figures by heart before Brom had the string back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:He looked back at the third stride. The eight were coming down off the lip, one at a time, without haste, and going into the standing grass on either side of the lane. After that there was only grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:It was dusk in earnest now. The lane was a grey stroke going down between two dark walls as high as his hip. The west had a green light in it still, and behind him the floor lay on the crown of the swell like a moon that had come down to rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:15:He said it low, for the file. His strides were yards, and he had paced this lane at noon behind a cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:19:He had been waiting for it, and it was still a bad thing to see. On the south side, a few yards out from the lane, the seed-heads bent and stood and bent again in a slow line that went along beside the file. When he turned his head they were doing the same on the north. No shape showed in it anywhere; there was only the grass leaning, a little at a time, at exactly the pace of a girl putting down the whole of her foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:31:They were careless. He turned the word over, and it fitted every count that he had. They had no cause to be anything else. Whatever walked over that line left its Path at a stone, and came on as a deer comes, or as a man with a stick; and in all the years that a pack had lain under that floor, no prey in this place had ever shouted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:39:It would not be enough to draw them off. They would come back to the lane when he fell quiet, or follow him to the stone and over it. On the far side of that stone there were four hurt people and the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:41:He lengthened his stride and came up past Brom's elbow, and along the edge of the lane to the small figure behind Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:77:At a hundred Lira's bad foot dragged, and she made no sound; he knew it only from how the healer's shoulder dropped to take her. At ninety Brom said, without turning, that there was something in the lane behind them, and Cael looked. One of the eight had come out onto the trodden grass thirty yards back, and was sitting there with its tongue out. It got up when they had gone on ten strides, and came after, and sat again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:He could see the stone. It was a paler thing at the lane's end, the size of a loaf. The mule stood beyond it with her long ears forward, hobbled, and in no fear at all; nothing in that grass wanted anything that she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:97:Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:107:He could let Wind have him. It would crack like a whip over the lane and set him down beside her with his feet locked for a count, and it would bring eight animals to the ground where Karis was lying, with him standing in the middle of them unable to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:There was a third thing, and it was already lifting. It had lifted at sunset behind the other eight, as it lifted at every dusk, with the one offer that it ever made. It did not put anything into the air. It was made to take something out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:113:Lira could, and Brom, and Karis from under the thing's jaw; and Seln, who was looking straight back up the lane, and whose own stair it had come off. Those four knew what he was and had kept it for years. There was no bowman on a bank. There was nobody else nearer than two days' walk, and a pack of dogs carries no tales to a ferry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:121:The hiss of the seed-heads against his coat stopped. That was how it came: the world went on, and he went out of it. His boots came down on trodden grass and gave him nothing back; he could not hear his own breath. He went wide of the lane on the south side, through stems that stood to his hip and bent for him and did not rustle, past the box on Brom's back and Brom's left shoulder. Brom's eye came round and slid over the place where he was, as an eye slides over one fence-post among forty, and then came back to it, since Brom knew. He passed Lira on her left hand, a yard out. Her head turned. She was looking at grass that leaned with nobody in it, and he saw her mouth open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:125:It was ten strides. On the tenth he let it go, with his hands already on the animal's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:147:He did not answer her. He set his feet in the trodden grass as he had watched her set hers ten thousand times, with the lane across him and his face to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:155:It broke over the lane as it had broken over a river, and the grass went down flat behind him in a fan. He was twenty feet north of five people, in stems to his hip, and his boots had come down left foot first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:157:The landing locked. *One*, and everything that he owned went dumb; there was a boy standing in a field with a knife, who could not have shifted a foot to save his life. He had priced that count before he spent it. The thrown one was on the south side, finding its legs, and the rest were strung along sixty yards of lane. The nearest of them was a good deal more than one count from his ankles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:159:All along both sides of the lane, the grass had stopped moving at Lira's pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:173:They had come up out of the grass: there were low grey shapes on both sides of the lane and in it, and every head was turned to him, and in every head there were two small green fires where the flame looked back. He counted the pairs. He could not have helped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:189:There was one thing more that he had refused at every doorway and coping since a river, and he let that off its hold with the rest. Anchor fixed nothing, for he had no point on that hill that he could afford to nail to the ground. It only looked, west, as a dog let off a chain looks first at the gate. It would be within ten feet of a thing before it could tell him what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:197:The rut ran up the swell toward the floor, a little south of east, and every stride that he took along it was a stride away from a lane with a lame girl on it. He put his back to five people and went up the hill the wrong way, with eight animals pouring into the channel behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:203:He laid the lane down the rut behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:205:It was a corridor of air the length of a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other, and everything in it went downhill at once. The pale grass stood along both lips of the rut with its seed heavy on it. The lane stripped it. Seed and chaff and dry stem went down that channel in a flood, as thick as meal out of a sack, straight into the faces of whatever was coming up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:He heard them meet it. He let the lane go, and paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:209:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:213:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:He had priced this count too, and the price was a bad one. The dark-backed one had not gone into the chaff. It had run the rut's rim. Whether a boy's two hands had taught it something in the lane he could not have said. It was the only one of the eight that had been on the right side of the channel when he left it, and it was three strides from him, and already in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:263:He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:275:It was Anchor. It had gone the whole length of that slope with its face to the west and found nothing that it could be sure of. Now it found nine things. They stood in a row directly across his road, a little more than a stride apart, to the right hand and the left, low on the ground, just beyond the stone. Not one of them gave any light. They were places that somebody had fixed and told to stay, as a bar of iron had been told to stay on that same line, three cords to the north. He knew the hand that had set them as he would have known its writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:277:She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:295:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:Beyond the stone the slope went up into the dark. At the top of it there was still a faint paleness where the floor lay, and between that and the lamp, along sixty yards of grass, ran a strip of black no wider than his hand, where a wall had stood for a count of six; the frost was coming down on it already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:Lira was on her back in the grass, and the healer knelt beside her. She had the stiff black linen off the arm already, a turn at a time, and laid in a heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:15:She put her hands round the arm, one above the place and one below, as she had put them round it at the head of a lane and had them come away empty. Her eyes shut. Her lips moved, and this time there was a count in them; he saw it go to four, and to five, and on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:"It's deep. It's the deepest thing I've done on a road. I'll be in it an hour, near enough, and I'll hear nothing that anybody says to me, so say it now." She jerked her chin at the dark. "You've a finding about what those things hunt. I've heard it at your fire more than once. A mend's a Path used, and this one's a Path used for an hour, at night, with seven of those somewhere in that grass. It's the loudest thing for a mile, and I can't do it quietly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with eight fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:41:"Mine's a calf, and an arm that's been struck. And what went up that hill with a light was me. I did it on purpose, to take them off the lane. I've no more to say about it tonight than that it was done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:61:He did not watch the arm. He had seen what was to be seen of that at the head of a lane, and he found that he had no wish to learn how it looked when the linen was off. A healer on a mountain had grown a little older while he looked at her, over a shin. It was more than a little now. The sweat came out along her cropped hair in the first minutes and ran, though the frost was coming down, and by the middle of the hour the skin under her eyes had gone the colour of a bruise; her breath came slowly, at the pace that she walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, by the light of a brass lamp, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it she had not expected, while a stranger's hands took six weeks out of her arm in an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left forearm went first. The ringing had gone out of the bone before he reached the stone, and something duller had come into its place, which spread; by the end of the first quarter he could not have told by feel where his fingers were. He set the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:75:The heaviest of them lay in him like wet sand. He had put three lanes down a ditch, and he could feel every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:The healer had not finished. She kept hold of the wrist, and she bent over Lira until her face was a hand's breadth away, and she spoke as she had spoken to Brom about a cuff, slowly, so that there should be no second meaning in any piece of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:"No. You were about to not ask, and I'd sooner say it than watch you." Oryn's voice had gone to a thread. "I'll tape it in the morning. I've nothing left to see it with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Seln brought them down, since he was the only one with two hands and nothing wrong with either, and he laid two on Lira and one on the healer where she had fallen. He cut the bread as well, a round loaf from the hold's oven, into six, with the same knife that had cut the sling; and he left the sixth piece on a pannier lid by Oryn's head, where she would find it before she found anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:He read them, and nodded. She took the leaf back and held the pencil over it, and he watched the point come down to the paper and lift, and come down. He knew what was in that small head. She had been ten strides in front of him with a hound's jaw across her fists, and she had seen a thing arrive that had not come; and after it a crack, and a light, and something that rang. She had nine questions at the least, and she had been waiting all her life for a source like this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:159:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:Across the pads of four fingers on each there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled, and in two of them it had gone deep. They had bled down into his cuffs and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:181:That was the camp. Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:*Nine hounds, inside. Two dead, both Brom's, with a healer's bow that he had never shot: one on the floor, at thirteen feet, with my hands on its head; one over the stone, at six. Seven out, north and south along the line's outside. Not seen after. No instruments were there. No price.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:203:*The read: far lip to the rut. One head turned for it. I had my eyes in the ground when Lira went up on the rim.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:205:*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:207:*Wind: a burst, north off the lane. The lock.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:*Ember: one point, on a knife, lane to stone. Nothing seen beyond ten feet of it. A blister where the hilt was.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:215:*Anchor: looking, nothing fixed. It showed me nine points before they lit.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:He went down the ten lines with the pencil's end and counted uses, taking the three lanes singly, and had twelve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:225:*Twelve. With the ford, thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:239:He read it over. It was the kind of sentence that Karis struck out of other people's notes, since it had a likeness in it. He let it stand, since he had nothing truer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:It was one word, and she had left him the whole width of it. She did not say which of them, or how many. She did not say that she had been looking at grass on her left hand, in a lane, with nobody in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:259:He waited for the next thing, and found that he was holding his breath as he had held it under a healer's hand. He had a great many sentences ready for her, and not one of them was an answer. He did not know why. He had not known at noon, and he had kept that from her at noon with a pencil going; and she, of all the people alive, had the right to ask him how long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:277:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*

