   133	A hound with its hind feet off the ground has nothing to bite from. It let go of two fists to find the earth again, and by then it was in the air; and it went over his hip and into the standing grass on the south side, a full yard out, and he heard it land.
   134	
   135	Karis lay with her fists under her chin and her mouth open. No sound was coming out of it. Her eyes were on him, and they were quite clear. They went to his hands, and to the place ten strides back where he had been standing, and returned.
   136	
   137	"Seln," he said. "Carry her. The stone. Don't stop for me."
   138	
   139	"What are you—" said Brom.
   140	
   141	"I'm going to make a noise. Walk."
   142	
   143	"With what?" said Lira.
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	He did not answer her. He set his feet in the trodden grass as he had watched her set hers ten thousand times, with the lane across him and his face to the north.
   148	
   149	He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
   150	
   151	He stopped holding.
   152	
   153	The air cracked.
   154	
   155	It broke over the lane as it had broken over a river, and the grass went down flat behind him in a fan. He was twenty feet north of five people, in stems to his hip, and his boots had come down left foot first.
   156	
   157	The landing locked. *One*, and everything that he owned went dumb; there was a boy standing in a field with a knife, who could not have shifted a foot to save his life. He had priced that count before he spent it. The thrown one was on the south side, finding its legs, and the rest were strung along sixty yards of lane. The nearest of them was a good deal more than one count from his ankles.
   158	
   159	All along both sides of the lane, the grass had stopped moving at Lira's pace.
   160	
   161	It stood. Then it bent, everywhere at once, toward him.
   162	
   163	The count was over, and he had his nine.
   164	
   165	He drew breath for Pressure. It was a deeper breath than a healer's tape allowed, and his left side told him so from the breastbone to the spine. It gathered under his ribs for the better part of that breath, as it had always gathered, slowly, where anything alive might feel it coming. There was no opponent to send it through. He sent it straight down.
   166	
   167	The ground thumped like a drum. The grass lay over in a ring ten yards across, and the force of it came back up through his heels and his knees, as it came back off anything that would not move.
   168	
   169	Ember was next. He gave it the one place where he could carry it, which was the tip of Lira's knife; and for the first time since the Line he did not stop at the setting. It lit. A seed of yellow stood on the steel, and then a flame as long as his thumb, burning on nothing, at the end of his arm.
   170	
   171	He could see them.
   172	
   173	They had come up out of the grass: there were low grey shapes on both sides of the lane and in it, and every head was turned to him, and in every head there were two small green fires where the flame looked back. He counted the pairs. He could not have helped it.
   174	
   175	Eight.
   176	
   177	Something bright and quick stirred in his right arm and asked what it had asked of every sill and rim and arris since the day it came to him. *Edge?*
   178	
   179	*Yes*, he told that one too. He had never said so before. Along the knife's cutting side a line of white stood out a hair clear of the steel, as a tall exact person had once made one stand on a lath by saying that it did; and it rang. There was nothing for it to cut. It sang all the same, thin and high, like a wet finger going round the rim of a glass.
   180	
   181	He had a light in his fist and a noise at the end of it, and he was the only thing on that whole hillside that had either.
   182	
   183	He ran north.
   184	
   185	Behind him he heard Lira's voice, once, and it was not his name; it was "Go!" and it was not said to him.
   186	
   187	They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
   188	
   189	There was one thing more that he had refused at every doorway and coping since a river, and he let that off its hold with the rest. Anchor fixed nothing, for he had no point on that hill that he could afford to nail to the ground. It only looked, west, as a dog let off a chain looks first at the gate. It would be within ten feet of a thing before it could tell him what was there.
   190	
   191	It was forty yards to the rut. He counted them, with the flame streaming back over his wrist and the edge singing. At the fortieth the ground went out from under his left foot, and he was in it to the knee.
   192	
   193	---
   194	
   195	West was the stone. That was why he turned east.
   196	
   197	The rut ran up the swell toward the floor, a little south of east, and every stride that he took along it was a stride away from a lane with a lame girl on it. He put his back to five people and went up the hill the wrong way, with eight animals pouring into the channel behind him.
   198	
   199	He had kept the heaviest thing for a place that would hold it.
   200	
   201	A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
   202	
   203	He laid the lane down the rut behind him.
   204	
   205	It was a corridor of air the length of a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other, and everything in it went downhill at once. The pale grass stood along both lips of the rut with its seed heavy on it. The lane stripped it. Seed and chaff and dry stem went down that channel in a flood, as thick as meal out of a sack, straight into the faces of whatever was coming up.
   206	
   207	He heard them meet it. He let the lane go, and paid.
   208	
   209	*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
   210	
   211	At the tenth count he laid the second.
   212	
   213	It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
   214	
   215	It opened again. He felt it go, like wet cloth parting, and after that every stride on that side had a hot place in it.
   216	
   217	*Eight. Nine. Ten.*
   218	
   219	He laid the third. It was as many as he had ever laid; he did not know what a fourth would do to him, and it was not the night to learn. The rut behind him was a river of chaff. Twelve counts, for the third. His own breath reached him late.
   220	
   221	He had counted his strides from the place where he dropped into the rut, and he was at a hundred and thirty when he heard it.
   222	
   223	"*Back!*"
   224	
   225	It came up the hill from the west, across two hundred yards of dark, and it was a voice that he had never in two years heard raised: mild, and exact, and pitched to carry. It was the word that five of them had said together at a stone, on the evening that they found the line.
   226	
   227	A breath after it there was another, higher, and cracked in the middle.
   228	
   229	"*Back!*"
   230	
   231	Two. He made five people out of two voices, running. Seln would not have called with Karis still on the wrong side of that stone, and Karis could not call. Lira had a healer under her arm. Brom he had to take on trust; Brom would be the last of them over, and would not spend breath on a word.
   232	
   233	They were out.
   234	
   235	He stopped running east.
   236	
   237	It was as near as he came that night to being caught. He turned in the rut, with the flame swinging, and they were there: grey, and close, coming up out of the chaff with their eyes shut to slits and their muzzles white with seed. The channel was full of them. He could not go back down it, and he could not stand.
   238	
   239	He gave Wind his feet a second time.
   240	
   241	It answered a beat late. He felt that as a man feels a stair that is not where he thought. Then the air broke, and he was out of the rut and over its southern lip, twenty feet down the open slope, with the knife still lit in his fist.
   242	
   243	The landing locked.
   244	
   245	*One.*
   246	
   247	He had priced this count too, and the price was a bad one. The dark-backed one had not gone into the chaff. It had run the rut's rim. Whether a boy's two hands had taught it something in the lane he could not have said. It was the only one of the eight that had been on the right side of the channel when he left it, and it was three strides from him, and already in the air.
   248	
   249	He could not move his feet. He could not have moved a finger.
   250	
   251	It hit him at the end of the count, and Compression took it.
   252	
   253	It took it late. For the first beat there was nothing between him and a hound's whole weight but the left forearm that he had thrown across his body, and he felt the bone of that arm ring from the wrist to the elbow like a struck rail. Then the thing that a fighter had once put through him, and left with him, came up through his frame and did what it was for. The rest of the blow went through his shoulder and his spine and his locked knees, and down into the hill.
   254	
   255	He stood. The hound came off him as it would have come off a wall, sideways, and rolled.
   256	
   257	His feet were his own again. He ran west.
   258	
   259	---
   260	
   261	It was two hundred yards to the stone, downhill. He ran it on a calf that was open, and a side that had been told *there* too many times to go on saying it.
   262	
   263	He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
   264	
   265	They were behind him. He could hear them come out of the rut, one after another, with a scrabbling of feet on its lip, and then the rushing in the grass. One sound was nearer than the rest, and gaining, and that was a thing with long legs, built for this.
   266	
   267	A grey shape went by on his right, as high as his hip, and it was a boulder. Twenty strides.
   268	
   269	"Cael!"
   270	
   271	That was Brom, from in front and below. Cael saw nothing of him. But Brom could see a light coming down a hill with a boy under it, and something low and fast a few yards behind the light.
   272	
   273	At ten feet from the stone the last of the nine told him what it had been looking for.
   274	
   275	It was Anchor. It had gone the whole length of that slope with its face to the west and found nothing that it could be sure of. Now it found nine things. They stood in a row directly across his road, a little more than a stride apart, to the right hand and the left, low on the ground, just beyond the stone. Not one of them gave any light. They were places that somebody had fixed and told to stay, as a bar of iron had been told to stay on that same line, three cords to the north. He knew the hand that had set them as he would have known its writing.
   276	
   277	She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
   278	
   279	He went over the stone between the fourth and the fifth.
   280	
   281	There was grass under him that was only grass, and a mule's shape, and a big man down on one knee a yard in front of him with a bow-stock at his cheek. Brom's eye was not on him. It was on something past his hip.
   282	
   283	The string slapped.
   284	
   285	Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
   286	
   287	"Seven," said Lira's voice, from somewhere on the ground.
   288	
   289	"Karis," said Cael. "Now."
   290	
   291	She was on her knees a stride from the stone, with Seln's hands under her arms to keep her upright. She had no voice to answer him with. Her two hands were out in front of her, shaking, and he saw the right one make the smallest movement that it had ever made.
   292	
   293	The line lit.
   294	
   295	It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
   296	
   297	The seven came to it.
   298	
   299	He saw them only as shapes against the fire, and only for as long as it burned. They had been strung out down two hundred yards of hill behind a light, running to a declaration, and the declaration had gone over a stone and gone out; for he had let the flame die and the edge fall silent when the string slapped, and had shut his hand on all nine. What stood in front of them now was a fence of fire with their own road under it.
   300	
   301	They split on it. The foremost flung left and the next flung right, and the rest went after one or the other, as thirty head of horn had gone on a mountain. They ran along the inside of that wall to its two ends. Then they were over the line, somewhere in the dark beyond the light, to the north and to the south; and the last that he knew of any of them was a rushing in the grass that went away along the line's outside in both directions, and thinned, and stopped.
   302	
   303	Karis held it for a count of six. He counted it for her. At six the wall went down all at once, and she sat back against Seln's knees with her hands open in her lap.
   304	
   305	It was full dark. The stars were out. He lay where he had fallen and went over it, since somebody had to.
   306	
   307	Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. One of them had an arm bitten through and an ankle gone for the third time, and one could not speak. One was looking at the fingers of both his hands by starlight. He himself had a calf laid open, and a forearm in which the feeling was going out like a tide, and nine things under his hand again that had not yet stopped shaking.
   308	
   309	There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.
   310	
   311	He could say what had been done, as Pike would have said it. A thing loud enough to be hunted had been made in the one place where nothing could be loud. It had taken a pack off five people who could not make a sound that the pack would hear, and a line of fire had kept it from following them out. That was the whole of the win.
   312	
   313	The pack had learned nothing from it that it could use. It had never needed to learn anything, and he did not suppose that it would begin now.
   314	
   315	And five people had watched him go up a hill with fire in his fist. Four of them had seen one thing more than the fifth had.
   316	
   317	Nobody spoke to him. Somebody was striking a flint.

