editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost. He had Lira's arm in it, and a throat, and eight fingers; he had his own leg, and a number in a book. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and the stop had taken three mendings in two days from seven gates that had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:"I have traced three things from his map, and no more. Here is the first water; those are his three strokes. Here is his square with a dot, which is the hold we slept at. And here is his day's measure, a tick to a day, so that what I draw shall be on his scale and not on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:219:He had drawn it in charcoal. Cael saw a row of short strokes going across the thin paper from left to right, and two days beyond a small square they bent, and went about a neat black ring, and came back.
