editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:It had ridden there since the ninety-first morning, folded once, between a letter in a narrow upright hand and a leaf out of a ledger. He opened it on his knee, and they heard it again as Pike had given it to a meadow: the recovery, and the instruments, and the rise where a road left its line. A hundred marks, in words, and again in a bracket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:277:"We looked very thoroughly," said Seln. "I have been asking myself since last night what a paper is for that sends a crew to fetch a thing which is not there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:They were in the loft, among the barley sacks, with the ladder drawn up after them. The two boxes that had waited there stood against the gable, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, had been set down between them and not yet opened. Below them the barn was dark. Across the yard there was one small window with no light in it, where a healer had gone to her bed at dusk like a person who has work in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:Lira had the sack nearest the light, with her right hand laid over the sling. Brom sat on the boards with his knees up and his two hands open on them, pale across the pads of the fingers. Karis had her notebook, and a throat the colour of an old apple; she had spoken twice that day, a word at a time, in a voice like a rasp on wood. Seln had placed himself by the ladder's head, where he could hear the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:11:"She runs the long reading at first light," said Cael. "On me, in the back room, with the door shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:"And none of you said what it was, and nor did I. I've had fifteen days in which to say it, and a ring; and I'm saying it now with one night to go, which is late." He looked at the flame. "It's the thing that I take them in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:It was a single leaf, and she had been two years in the making of it. It had a curve on it, for how quickly each of the nine had come, and a column for what each had cost him afterward; and at its head, in her small exact hand, stood three words with a rule under every one. She had never in his hearing called it more than a model. He had its three words by heart, and so had the others, since she had read them out on the night that the eighth was priced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:29:"So I've laid tomorrow beside her page, a piece at a time. It wants to be sustained. She says twenty minutes, with both her hands on me, and I've never stood a bout that was a quarter as long. It wants stakes, on both sides. Mine are that I'm three days off that floor, with every one of the nine still shaken by it. Hers are that she's spent three mendings on us and she's late at the top of her round, and she means to lay out a fourth on a boy who has given her nothing four times. And it wants to be in earnest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:59:"She doesn't know, and I don't, since there's been nothing to try it on. Every one of the nine came out of a fight of some kind. There's never been a person who did a real thing to me for a long time, at a cost, who wasn't standing on the other side of a floor." He touched the second leaf. "If she's right, tomorrow is the first morning of my life on which it could happen with nobody struck. And if it does, I shall have a piece of what a healer reads with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:81:"The first is that if I tell her, it's a different morning. She would be reading a patient that she'd been warned about. She might keep some of herself back, to see; or she might go in looking for the very thing I'd named. I don't believe that either of those is the whole of her, and the page says that it has to be real. I'd have turned it into a trial."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"I said something," said Lira. "In a lane, when I'd never heard of it either. You told me on the day you knew, and I didn't run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:109:He had known the answer since the fork, and had gone round it for a day and a half as a carter goes round a ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:167:"That it is the nearest, and that it is not the thing itself. A person who writes down only the first has begun to forgive himself." The mild eyes rested on him. "You wrote such a leaf for the captain, the night before, and you did not tell him, and you have carried that. But he had asked you for a fight, and a person who asks for a fight knows that it will cost him something. She believes that she is the only one in that room who will be doing anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:171:He heard that it was the weakest thing he had said all night, and he said it regardless, since it was true and he wished to have it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:173:"I'm not to make one come. That's the first rule, and it's kept; I never put this in her mouth. She said it herself, at a ford, before I had so much as thought of it. *When you're not broken, I run the long one.* She put a day on her route for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:187:"I'm going to do it," he said. "I've heard every one of you, and I think that you're right. I am going to lie down on that table in the morning and say nothing, and tell her the minute that it's over. I want you to know that I chose it with all of this in front of me. Nobody talked me into it, and nobody was kept from talking me out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:189:"Then enter it tonight," said Seln. "By your own rule: before, and at its full weight. An entry made tomorrow will have learned how the morning went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:203:He did not put it under the day's heading. He had written two such leaves before this one: the first in a week of rain, with four people's initials at its foot, and the second at a sill, on the night before a bout with a friend who was not to be told. He turned to a clean page for the third, and began it with the two words that were the point of the whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:*Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:*The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:221:It was not a good page. He had hoped a little that it would look better written; that somewhere between the pencil and the paper it would turn into the kind of thing that a careful person does. It had not. It read as what it was: a boy wanted something, and had found that he could not have it honestly, and was going to take it and pay afterward; and he had set down the price first, in a hand that would be dry by morning, so that he could never claim to have been surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:The bead went out. He lay down between two sacks of barley with the book under his hand; and in the dark Wind leaned a little toward where Lira was breathing, and the heaviest of the nine shifted, like a sea a long way off, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:249:The ninth had drawn itself in very small. This was the first light, and not the last; that one came up at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:251:She had told him in a yard to hold nothing. He had spent a hundred days in learning to refuse the nine, a stride at a time; and now he was to lie here for twenty minutes and neither refuse them nor let them go, and he did not know the name of that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:275:It lay exactly over the place where a small bright thing had gathered on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. He had gone looking for that place on a hillside five days ago, with five people abreast of him, and had found it dark. Her palm was on it now. She could not have known that; it was only where a healer puts a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:Beyond the stone the slope went up into the dark. At the top of it there was still a faint paleness where the floor lay, and between that and the lamp, along sixty yards of grass, ran a strip of black no wider than his hand, where a wall had stood for a count of six; the frost was coming down on it already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:She said it to the arm, on a breath, and that was all the notice that she gave it; she had waited thirty rounds to learn what a line did to her hands, and learned it at noon, and tonight she had no time to spare for having them back. She opened her eyes and looked up at five people, and spoke quickly, as she had spoken on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:"It's deep. It's the deepest thing I've done on a road. I'll be in it an hour, near enough, and I'll hear nothing that anybody says to me, so say it now." She jerked her chin at the dark. "You've a finding about what those things hunt. I've heard it at your fire more than once. A mend's a Path used, and this one's a Path used for an hour, at night, with seven of those somewhere in that grass. It's the loudest thing for a mile, and I can't do it quietly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with eight fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:33:"Ninety-sixth day. Dark. The west side of the stone. There were nine. Two are dead, one on the floor with a bolt in it that we'll not see again, and one there." He did not point. "Seven went out when the wall lit, north and south, along the outside of the line. I didn't see where they went after. Did anyone?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"Lira's arm, and her ankle. Karis's throat. Brom's hands. Six bolts, at noon: three in the sleeve, one on the string, one a yard from that stone, and one gone. The box is whole, and there are eleven sheets in it and on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:59:He held the lantern for an hour, and for the whole of it he watched her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:61:He did not watch the arm. He had seen what was to be seen of that at the head of a lane, and he found that he had no wish to learn how it looked when the linen was off. A healer on a mountain had grown a little older while he looked at her, over a shin. It was more than a little now. The sweat came out along her cropped hair in the first minutes and ran, though the frost was coming down, and by the middle of the hour the skin under her eyes had gone the colour of a bruise; her breath came slowly, at the pace that she walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, by the light of a brass lamp, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it she had not expected, while a stranger's hands took six weeks out of her arm in an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:67:He had an hour, too, in which the bills came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:71:His hands were cold, both of them, with a cold that the night did not account for. He knew whose that was. It had come down a stair with a boy, long ago, in another person's life, and it had taken something out of the air for ten strides and kept a little of him for its trouble.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:81:He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped; the wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the black linen in the heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:83:Seven animals were somewhere in that dark which hunted the very thing that a square woman was doing with her eyes shut, and they did not come. He did not know why. He set it beside a great many other things that he did not know, and held the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:97:Brom said something to the dark that was not a word. Cael found that the lantern was shaking, and that it was not his dead arm that shook it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:It was Lira's own knife that Cael gave her, since it was the one in his belt. She set it to the hem of a grey blanket and drew, and the wool dented under it and did not part. She tried it again nearer the hilt. Then she held the blade to the lantern and ran her thumb along it, squarely, as nobody would have dared to do that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:111:Nobody answered her, and she did not seem to want an answer. She held out her hand to the south, and a narrow dull knife came into it out of the dark, haft first. That one cut. She took a strip off the blanket a hand wide and as long as her two arms, and passed it under Lira's elbow and round her neck, and tied it short, so that the mended arm lay across the breast with its hand under the opposite collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She said it as Brom had once said the hour of a week, to have it exact. Then her eyes went down the length of herself to the great boot, which lay on its side in the grass with the foot still in it, and came up again; and she shut her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:"No. You were about to not ask, and I'd sooner say it than watch you." Oryn's voice had gone to a thread. "I'll tape it in the morning. I've nothing left to see it with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:127:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything: the healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning, Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:Across the pads of four fingers on each there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled, and in two of them it had gone deep. They had bled down into his cuffs and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:175:"Morning. Wash it. I've nothing tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:193:*Ninety-sixth day, night. West of the stone, on the old road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:197:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: eight fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:229:It stood where the word *one* had stood on forty-three nights. He had shut the book at a far lip because he did not know what would be true by dark; and here was a true number, and a large one, and there was not a unit in it that had not been paid for on somebody's body, his own or another's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:It was not the whole. He looked at that squarely, with the pencil lifted. There were things that he had done at noon, by himself, a cord north of this stone and on a floor, which nothing had seen and which he had written of only to say that he had told nobody. They were not in the twelve. He would not put them into a count that he could not yet explain, and he would not pretend to the page that they had not happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:233:*Not in that number: noon. It's on the leaf before this. It gets a line of its own when I can say what it was for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:235:There was one thing more, and it was the only part of the day that he could write as plainly as a width.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:259:He waited for the next thing, and found that he was holding his breath as he had held it under a healer's hand. He had a great many sentences ready for her, and not one of them was an answer. He did not know why. He had not known at noon, and he had kept that from her at noon with a pencil going; and she, of all the people alive, had the right to ask him how long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:263:She said it up into the dark. There was no hardness in it. It was a thing put by on a shelf, with its face outward, where neither of them could pretend that it was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:273:He could not make it. He had the fact, which was that nine things had answered him in a place where a healer could not count. He had nothing to put after the fact. And he knew what became of a thing that was written down before anybody understood it, since it had been done to him: a clerk had found no tier for a boy, and had put a word in capitals where the tier should go, and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was. A file stopped the asking. He would not do that to this, and he would not hide it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:275:It was the better part of two hours before he opened the book again; and he wrote by starlight, large, one sentence to a line, as Karis had written at this same stone on the night before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:277:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:It lay a yard west of the stone, where it had come down, with the frost of one night on it. Seln had passed a turn of the long cord round its hind legs. The healer brought the mule up, and the mule looked at what she was to draw, and laid her ears along her neck, and was led round twice before she would stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Karis had the sleeve. She had taken charge of it at first light, when Brom was told what he might not do, and she slid the bolt in beside the rest and showed them the mouth of the leather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:19:Cael counted, since she could not say it. There were three that had never left the sleeve, and one that had sat on a string all night and not been wanted, and one out of a hound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:29:He could not cut it. He sat on a pannier with his hands on his knees and told Seln where to put the knife, and Seln cut, without expression, as he would have taken dictation. They had three loaves of Thornwater's, and a cheese of the healer's, and five onions. It was two days to the fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:31:"Tonight. Tomorrow at noon, and tomorrow night. Then the morning after, and we're in by dark." Brom pointed. "That's four. Six to a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:49:Nobody said *yours*. It was the one word that Cael listened for through the whole of that day, and he did not hear it from any of them. Lira had said it once, under the stars, and put it on a shelf; and the other three seemed to have agreed among themselves, without a hand raised, that it was hers to take down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Throat and fingers mended at noon; nothing came. My calf read and not mended: the fourth time she's found nothing. The dead one drawn off south. The bolt cut out: five in the sleeve. Bread in sixths, by Brom, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:63:That night, the ninety-seventh, Lira lay on her back with one arm tied across her chest and found that she could not get comfortable round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:69:She had said the whole of it over to herself that morning, to have it exact, as she would have got a card by heart. It was the last part that she kept coming back to. She had spent her life catching things. A lath, a wrist, a rail at the top of a climb; a small pale person who never looked where she put her feet. Her left hand went out for things before she had been told that they were falling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She left the ankle alone. She had asked a healer once, from the back of a mule, and had been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed; and last night that same healer had watched her not ask and had answered regardless. That made twice told. She would not make it twice asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:93:It was hers. That was the whole of it, and she made herself look straight at it. He had had it off her before either of them knew that he could, and for two years it had been the one that he was shyest of, since it was hers and she was standing there. She had stood at that stone at noon, yesterday, and gone up on her toes, and asked for all of it with a panel's worth of wanting; and she had been given a step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:95:Seven hours later it had gone off in his feet like a whip, twenty feet north, inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:That left *since when*. She had the right to it. She could have had it out of him last night with one more word; he had been sitting there waiting for it with his breath held, and he would have told her everything, badly, in the wrong order, with his arm dead and hers tied, because she had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:119:Cael had the last watch, on the ninety-eighth morning, and so he was the only one who saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:145:He had the book open. He had the day's number written at the head of a clean leaf, and nothing under it. He had entered every crossing of that line since the ninety-fifth evening: five people abreast, and Karis's nine in the dark, and each of the trials in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:155:They walked west on the ninety-eighth, and Lira went the whole way on the flat of her foot, since she had been told to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:She had not known how much of walking was done with the hands. Her balance was all on one side. When the wind pushed her she put out the right and nothing answered it from the left; when she stepped over a rut she felt the tied arm start, under the grey wool, and stop against the knot. It did that forty times in the first hour. It was like walking beside a dog that keeps going for the same gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:233:They all looked at it. In ninety-eight days of road nobody had seen such a thing come off one of their fires.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:239:"I tell myself that every night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:243:*Finding. A method correctly dictated for ninety-eight days and never once followed by its author. Tested: one cake. I am entering it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:He looked round the fire at five faces. Every one of them had watched a thing go north out of a lane that they had been certain could not happen, and had walked beside him for a day without a word about it. They had not gone quiet, or careful. The big man had let another person make his bread sooner than leave the others without, and the small one had made a joke of it on paper, and Lira had told a carter's boot what she thought of it. It was the same crew that had sat at a fire above a wash, with a healer added, who was looking at her own heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:257:He had not known that it could be. He had supposed, at a far lip with his pencil lifted, that whatever was true by dark would cost him this; and it had cost a good deal, on other people's bodies, and it had not cost this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:265:By the second noon the leg had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:269:It had opened on the ninety-sixth evening, in a ditch, with nothing under his soles. This was the middle of the ninety-ninth day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:It was a mile to the fork. He walked it at a mule's shoulder with a whole leg, and for the first time in fifteen days he let himself look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:She would lay both hands on him in the morning, and all that she was, for as long as it took. She would do it in earnest, since she did nothing in any other way. He had felt that ninth thing come to him at the end of a bout with a friend who had asked only to be shown straight; he knew, as well as he knew anything about himself, what her morning was likely to be made of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:309:He got it over his head with one arm and the other, which had come back to him on the road as far as the wrist. He stood by the well's coping in the last of the sun with five bands of linen round him that he had hated on the eighty-sixth day, and measured on the eighty-seventh, and since a certain evening had not once had the leisure to think about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:313:She took the first band at his breastbone, where its end was, and drew it off round his side to the spine in one pull, as she had laid it on. It came with a sound like a page tearing. The other four followed. He had expected to feel something give, and he felt only cold air on a place that had not had any for eighteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:319:He drew it in. He went past the place where a strip of pitch had told him *there* some four thousand times a day, and nothing told him anything. He had forgotten that there was so much room in him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:321:"Twenty-one days. A rib is six weeks. These are done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:323:"You said they'd be done on the ninety-ninth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:"I said the rule was twenty-one days. I'd have laid money on sooner, and I don't lay money." She was rolling the dead tape onto its peeled stick. "Leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:329:"That opened three nights back. It's closed. Four days, counting the one it went on." She let his leg go. "I've a man in this hold who cut his shin on a scythe at the start of the harvest and it's weeping yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:365:"Not one of you is worse than when I left, and one's better and won't care to hear it. I'll do what wants doing after noon tomorrow. I've a thing in the morning that needs all of me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:367:They got up, the five of them, and went back to whatever they had been doing; nobody on that bench had ever argued with her about a day. Cael stood by the well with his shirt in his hand and the cold on his ribs, and watched five people give up a morning to him that not one of them knew they were giving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:369:It was the same yard. He had not looked at it when he came in. There was the sweep of the well, and the cart-yard, and a boy forking straw into the barn under the loft where two boxes waited; the geese went by in a line, and somebody in the long house was scouring a pot. He had gone out of this place five days ago and stood on a floor that matched no stone in the world, and held a hound's head, and carried a light down a hill. Nothing here had altered by so much as a goose, and he found that he had been counting on that without knowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:It was dusk in earnest now. The lane was a grey stroke going down between two dark walls as high as his hip. The west had a green light in it still, and behind him the floor lay on the crown of the swell like a moon that had come down to rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:15:He said it low, for the file. His strides were yards, and he had paced this lane at noon behind a cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:29:At a ford, seven of their kind had learned inside a quarter of an hour to leave a silent body alone. These had learned nothing whatever since sunset, with one of their own lying dead on their own floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:39:It would not be enough to draw them off. They would come back to the lane when he fell quiet, or follow him to the stone and over it. On the far side of that stone there were four hurt people and the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:49:"Along the line. The stone in the middle, and as far each way as nine will reach. Set them and leave them dark. When the last of us is over, join them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:65:She turned her head and looked at him. It was nearly dark, and he could see only the pale shape of her face. He thought that she had heard something that was not about the order of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:87:It was the dark-backed one. It came out low and from the flank, at the smallest thing in the file, with none of the idleness that it had shown on a lip. He had time to think that Karis walked alone, and that he had been a fool not to see what that would look like from the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:There was a third thing, and it was already lifting. It had lifted at sunset behind the other eight, as it lifted at every dusk, with the one offer that it ever made. It did not put anything into the air. It was made to take something out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:113:Lira could, and Brom, and Karis from under the thing's jaw; and Seln, who was looking straight back up the lane, and whose own stair it had come off. Those four knew what he was and had kept it for years. There was no bowman on a bank. There was nobody else nearer than two days' walk, and a pack of dogs carries no tales to a ferry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:127:The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:149:He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:177:Something bright and quick stirred in his right arm and asked what it had asked of every sill and rim and arris since the day it came to him. *Edge?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:219:He laid the third. It was as many as he had ever laid; he did not know what a fourth would do to him, and it was not the night to learn. The rut behind him was a river of chaff. Twelve counts, for the third. His own breath reached him late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:225:It came up the hill from the west, across two hundred yards of dark, and it was a voice that he had never in two years heard raised: mild, and exact, and pitched to carry. It was the word that five of them had said together at a stone, on the evening that they found the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:237:It was as near as he came that night to being caught. He turned in the rut, with the flame swinging, and they were there: grey, and close, coming up out of the chaff with their eyes shut to slits and their muzzles white with seed. The channel was full of them. He could not go back down it, and he could not stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:He had priced this count too, and the price was a bad one. The dark-backed one had not gone into the chaff. It had run the rut's rim. Whether a boy's two hands had taught it something in the lane he could not have said. It was the only one of the eight that had been on the right side of the channel when he left it, and it was three strides from him, and already in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:263:He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:277:She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:295:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:They split on it. The foremost flung left and the next flung right, and the rest went after one or the other, as thirty head of horn had gone on a mountain. They ran along the inside of that wall to its two ends. Then they were over the line, somewhere in the dark beyond the light, to the north and to the south; and the last that he knew of any of them was a rushing in the grass that went away along the line's outside in both directions, and thinned, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:305:It was full dark. The stars were out. He lay where he had fallen and went over it, since somebody had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:21:The first was pale, nearly white along the spine, so that against that floor he could see little of it but the legs. The heaviest had a dark stripe from its neck to the root of its tail. Two smaller ones came over shoulder to shoulder and stayed so. There was one long in the leg, which moved like a thing built for running, and one grey to the eyes, which walked a little way onto the white and looked about it and lay down. Three more he could not have told from each other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:They did not hurry, and it was that which he liked least. They spread out among the eleven stones as a crew spreads out in its own yard at the end of a day. One sat and scratched. The pale one stood at the middle of the round, by the place where a cord had ended, with its nose down on the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:Behind him the slope fell away to the east, and the line on that side was nearer by twenty-six yards. It was also the hounds' own door. There were three runs across it and not a stride of it that any of the six had walked; the grass stood hip-high the whole way down, the ground was steep, and in a quarter of an hour it would be dark. If they came out alive on that side, they would be half a circle of open country from a mule. The west was a lane trodden flat by their own feet, with a stone at the end of it that five of them could find with their eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:"I looked at it at noon and left it lying. I'd come to find out about my hands, and I didn't want anything in them." Her mouth went hard. "That was a healer's reason. Six bolts, in a sleeve on the stock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:61:He went down off the trodden earth into the grass, stooping, and at his tenth stride there was grass and no Seln. He had done as much at noon, to amuse them, for a count of three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:71:"It's eleven sheets and her whole afternoon. It's the only thing we're bringing down off this hill." Brom shifted the plaited strap an inch across his chest, and took the bill in both hands like a quarterstaff, with the hook uppermost. "It weighs what it weighed at noon. I tried it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:117:The dark-backed one came next, forty yards farther round. It was the heaviest of the nine, and it came to the very same kind of place, at Brom's right hand, and stood, and brought its weight forward in the very same way; and Cael said "Now," and the haft was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:167:"You told me why, too. It's new, and it wants time. It's had sixteen days." Lira had her eyes on the nearer of the pair. "If it goes, mend it at the stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:It did not snap. It closed on the arm between the wrist and the elbow, as a door closes on a hand, and its jaws stayed shut. She was on her back, and he saw her do then what he had seen her do at a stone at noon: she gathered the whole of herself, heels and hips and shoulders, and asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:193:At noon it had bought her a step. It bought her a yard of floor now, sliding, with the animal's forefeet sliding after her and her arm in its mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:255:He straightened his back, and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch with a sound like a gate-latch. When he took his hands away there was a dark line across the pads of four fingers, and he wiped them down his breeches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:313:Then she looked at Cael. In that light her face was all planes, and there was a thing in it that he had not seen there at noon, when a count stopped in her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:319:The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:He lay very still. There was a beat in it. It was not his heart, which went a good deal faster, and it was not his breath. It was slower than either and it did not vary, and he knew it before he had found the word for where he knew it from. He had walked beside it for six days down a valley, at a mule's shoulder, with five bands of linen teaching him where his own edge was. It was the pace that she kept on a road. She was going through him at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:45:He had supposed that it would be like the talk at a bench, short and to the purpose. It was nothing of the kind. It was the low running murmur of a person who is feeling her way down a passage in the dark and saying what her hands meet, less to be heard than to keep herself from being lost; and he lay under it and counted, and tried to do as he had been told, which was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:"And the girl's goes by so fast that there's nothing to feel but the draught of it. You put your hand in and it's gone past already. That's what a Wind is like, under the hands. I had hold of hers for an hour at that stone and I'd not swear that I ever touched the bottom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:93:The heaviest of them she passed last, low down, under everything. He had felt it shift in a loft the night before, and it had been restless since a ditch; it had three lanes still in it, like a sea with a swell left over from weather a long way off. He waited for it to rise at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:*A hundred and forty-four*, he counted. Twelve minutes. He lay with his neck loose and all nine doors standing as they stood, and for the first time since a morning in a windowless room there was somebody in the house besides himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:103:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:105:They had all been afraid of him. He had first seen it on the morning of his Kindling, and had not had the word for it then. It had looked like anger, mostly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:119:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was not weaker. It had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge. She looked for it under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:155:It stood at his breastbone, under her palm, like a hand held out in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:159:That was the first thing that he was sure of. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for a hundred days, and he was better at that one act than at anything else he did. He had only to say no, at the first stride, as he had said it on a gravel bar. Her current would go by the empty place and find it shut, as it had found the ninth; she would come to the end of her twenty minutes and lift her hands and tell him that there was nothing, as before. He would get down off her table with what he had lain down on it with. Nothing would have been taken from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:210:He looked up at her. He had not done that since she shut her eyes. Her face hung over him, upside down, grey, with the sweat standing on it and the cropped hair dark; and the crease had come between her brows, the one that she wore over a sum that had come out oddly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:236:He had a hundred days of road behind him in which he had been asked what he was by nobody. He had thought, on a shelf above a valley, that this was freedom, and had written it down. He lay now under the hands of the one person past the Line who had tried to find out without a seal, and who had just paid for the trying with something that he did not yet know the size of; and he had a leaf in a loft that said what he owed.

