   122	
   123	Then she stepped across.
   124	
   125	He went still. She was one stride inside, and there were seven animals somewhere that he had not seen since a wall went down. He looked past her, at the grass, and kept his eyes moving over it; and since he could not have helped it, he counted.
   126	
   127	The first time she only stood, with her palms up, and stepped back.
   128	
   129	The second time she had two fingers of her right hand on her own left wrist, and her lips moving. He saw them stop as her boot came down. She stood so for a breath, and came out, and they began again before her heel had left the ground.
   130	
   131	She went over the third time by inches, as Karis had, sliding one foot; and the fourth time backward, with her eyes on the camp. The fifth time she shut her eyes and walked at the stone from six strides off, counting aloud, quite softly, and he heard the count break. She opened her eyes then and looked down, to see where her feet were. They were where the stone said that they ought to be. She did it again from farther back, and that was six.
   132	
   133	For the seventh she fetched the mule.
   134	
   135	She led the animal up until its forefeet were against the stone, and laid her whole hand on the warm neck under the mane, and stood with her head bent, reading whatever it is that a healer reads in a beast. Then, keeping her hand where it was, she stepped over, so that she stood inside with her arm stretched back across the line.
   136	
   137	She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
   138	
   139	The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
   140	
   141	She came out. She rubbed her hands together slowly, as she did when they had been in cold water, and she looked across the grass and saw him sitting with a book on his knee.
   142	
   143	Neither of them spoke. She held his eye for as long as it takes to count three. Then she went to her panniers.
   144	
   145	He had the book open. He had the day's number written at the head of a clean leaf, and nothing under it. He had entered every crossing of that line since the ninety-fifth evening: five people abreast, and Karis's nine in the dark, and each of the trials in its row.
   146	
   147	He looked at the pencil. It was a finding. She had made it eight times over, by a method, and it was about the line; Karis would have given a week's paper for it.
   148	
   149	It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
   150	
   151	He shut the book on a leaf with one line.
