editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:He lay very still. There was a beat in it. It was not his heart, which went a good deal faster, and it was not his breath. It was slower than either and it did not vary, and he knew it before he had found the word for where he knew it from. He had walked beside it for six days down a valley, at a mule's shoulder, with five bands of linen teaching him where his own edge was. It was the pace that she kept on a road. She was going through him at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:29:He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait. An assessor's panel had read him across a floor and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This had a gait. It favoured the right side by a hair, as she did, over a raw heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:55:"Your big one's are like a wall. Both sides, straight up, as if somebody had built them. I could have leaned on them. That's a Skin, and I'd know one through a blanket."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:69:He was at a hundred and eight. That was nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:103:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:115:He had been a case to a great many people. He had never before been a patient.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:141:"Eleven years," said Oryn. "I've never had my hands in something and not known what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:They were in the loft, among the barley sacks, with the ladder drawn up after them. The two boxes that had waited there stood against the gable, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, had been set down between them and not yet opened. Below them the barn was dark. Across the yard there was one small window with no light in it, where a healer had gone to her bed at dusk like a person who has work in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:29:"So I've laid tomorrow beside her page, a piece at a time. It wants to be sustained. She says twenty minutes, with both her hands on me, and I've never stood a bout that was a quarter as long. It wants stakes, on both sides. Mine are that I'm three days off that floor, with every one of the nine still shaken by it. Hers are that she's spent three mendings on us and she's late at the top of her round, and she means to lay out a fourth on a boy who has given her nothing four times. And it wants to be in earnest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:37:"Ephram was trying to beat me. He wasn't trying to hurt me, and the notice knew the difference; it said so, in two words that I'd never seen on one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:*Ephram fought you in earnest and the notice said non-hostile. This is earnest and it isn't a fight at all.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:83:Karis's pencil made a small mark on the stub, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:89:He said it quite gently, as he had said it at a stake to a small person holding up a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:"I know that it's never shown, and I've looked. Lira was no slower in the week after Ardenmere, and you were no softer; Karis set sixteen points for a panel inside a month of hers. Not one of the people that they came out of has ever said that they felt it go." He turned his palm up. "But every one of them was whole when it happened, and fighting. She will be spending. It costs her a mending to do this at all; so if it comes, it comes out of a purse that she has open, and I've nothing whatever to tell me what that does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:*The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:275:It lay exactly over the place where a small bright thing had gathered on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. He had gone looking for that place on a hillside five days ago, with five people abreast of him, and had found it dark. Her palm was on it now. She could not have known that; it was only where a healer puts a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:It lay a yard west of the stone, where it had come down, with the frost of one night on it. Seln had passed a turn of the long cord round its hind legs. The healer brought the mule up, and the mule looked at what she was to draw, and laid her ears along her neck, and was led round twice before she would stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:15:He stood there. Seln cut the bolt out first, since there would be no getting at it afterward. He did it with the narrow knife and his back turned to the rest of them, and it took him very little time; when he straightened he had a short thick shaft in his hand with the iron unbent, and he wiped it on the grass and held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Karis had the sleeve. She had taken charge of it at first light, when Brom was told what he might not do, and she slid the bolt in beside the rest and showed them the mouth of the leather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:33:"Five onions," said Lira, from her blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:37:He had Seln lay the pieces out on the lid of the middle box, four rows, and Cael saw the healer's eyes go along the first of them. She had said at a stake that six was wrong. There were six heaps in that row, of a size, and the sixth had not been set a little apart, as a guest's is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:It was a strange afternoon. They spoke of a good many things. Lira asked how far a mule could smell a dead dog, and the healer told her. Brom wished to know whether a bow was meant to pull to the left or whether that was him. Karis wrote that she would open the box under a roof and not before, since there was wind, and held it up; and they agreed that there was wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Throat and fingers mended at noon; nothing came. My calf read and not mended: the fourth time she's found nothing. The dead one drawn off south. The bolt cut out: five in the sleeve. Bread in sixths, by Brom, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:55:*Seln carried maps once that had round holes in them. One of the holes is here. The iron is the registry's. The paper was a question, and we answered it by coming out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:65:It did not hurt. That was the stupid part. She had been bitten to the bone at dusk and by midnight she had a forearm with eight small pink marks on it, as neat as stitching, and when she moved the fingers they moved. It was a perfectly good arm. It was not hers yet. It belonged to a square woman who was asleep six feet away with her mouth open, and it would go on belonging to her until there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:She let herself have that, for the length of a few breaths, since nobody else was going to give it to her. Twenty yards on a thing as round as a rail, in a boot like a bucket, on a leg that she had been forbidden to run on, with two dogs under her heels and one beside her knee; and she had been pulling away from all three. There had been nothing in it but legs. She had felt thirteen. She had felt the lanes at Fenmark come up under her as if they had only been waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She left the ankle alone. She had asked a healer once, from the back of a mule, and had been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed; and last night that same healer had watched her not ask and had answered regardless. That made twice told. She would not make it twice asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:The healer got up in the grey. She did not go to the mule. She walked the ten yards to the stone by herself, past the place where a dead thing had lain, and stood on the near side of it with her boots together, as she had stood on the first evening with her hands held out over nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:133:For the seventh she fetched the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:137:She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:149:It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:169:The big fool was behind her with the box, and his hands in two clean rags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:177:"You've got his other boot up there on the pack. If you say one word about my putting it on to match, I'll have it off that mule and you'll wear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:201:The healer had not broken step. She was ten yards ahead, on the low side of the mule, and she had her head turned on her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:207:It was not praise. Lira knew what praise sounded like; she had been given it on painted floors by people with slates. It was a count, said by a person who kept no book, and she found that she would sooner have had it than a sheet of figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:213:He had looked across the mule at her when Karis fell. He had seen the hand come out, and seen it go back. He had not said anything, and he had not looked away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:225:"More water than that. It wants to be like a wet clay. Now fold it; no, toward you. You're not folding a letter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:227:"I have folded a great many letters," said Seln. He had flour to the wrists, and his face was as mild as milk. "I had not understood that it was a disadvantage."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:It was a mile to the fork. He walked it at a mule's shoulder with a whole leg, and for the first time in fifteen days he let himself look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:She was there before they were. She had her sleeves rolled and a basin on her hip, and she watched them come up the last hundred yards as she had watched five come in from a ford: a head at a time, with her lips. He saw her eyes stop on a strip of grey blanket round a girl's neck. They went to a throat with a yellow mark across it, and to two rags, and a boy who walked as though he had been put together carefully. They went last to the healer, who was whole, and to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:293:"There are six blankets," said Seln. He had them rolled on his shoulder. "One is a hand narrower than it went out. I regret it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:327:He put his boot up on the coping. She unwound her own linen and turned the calf to the light. There was a seam down it, pale and dry, a hand long, lying over two older marks; she ran her thumb the length of it and did not shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:363:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:369:It was the same yard. He had not looked at it when he came in. There was the sweep of the well, and the cart-yard, and a boy forking straw into the barn under the loft where two boxes waited; the geese went by in a line, and somebody in the long house was scouring a pot. He had gone out of this place five days ago and stood on a floor that matched no stone in the world, and held a hound's head, and carried a light down a hill. Nothing here had altered by so much as a goose, and he found that he had been counting on that without knowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:The spark took on the third stroke. Karis's small brass lantern had come out of the middle box, and when its flame stood up Cael saw the camp that six people had made without meaning to. They were ten yards west of the grey stone, on the old line of the road, with the mule standing over them and her ears laid back. A long grey thing lay between them and the stone, where it had come down out of the air, and nobody looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with eight fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:33:"Ninety-sixth day. Dark. The west side of the stone. There were nine. Two are dead, one on the floor with a bolt in it that we'll not see again, and one there." He did not point. "Seven went out when the wall lit, north and south, along the outside of the line. I didn't see where they went after. Did anyone?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"Lira's arm, and her ankle. Karis's throat. Brom's hands. Six bolts, at noon: three in the sleeve, one on the string, one a yard from that stone, and one gone. The box is whole, and there are eleven sheets in it and on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:77:Under all of that the calf burned, slowly, in its own blood. He looked at none of it for long. He had opened a purse and counted out what was in it, and here was the reckoning, an item at a time, on his own body; and every item was smaller than the one that the healer had her hands on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:81:He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped; the wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the black linen in the heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:87:It was closed. That was all that he let himself see: skin, where there had been none, and on it two short rows of small pink marks, one row on the upper side and one beneath, each mark no bigger than a barleycorn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:105:"Blanket. One of the hold's. And a knife."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:It was Lira's own knife that Cael gave her, since it was the one in his belt. She set it to the hem of a grey blanket and drew, and the wool dented under it and did not part. She tried it again nearer the hilt. Then she held the blade to the lantern and ran her thumb along it, squarely, as nobody would have dared to do that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:111:Nobody answered her, and she did not seem to want an answer. She held out her hand to the south, and a narrow dull knife came into it out of the dark, haft first. That one cut. She took a strip off the blanket a hand wide and as long as her two arms, and passed it under Lira's elbow and round her neck, and tied it short, so that the mended arm lay across the breast with its hand under the opposite collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:127:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:131:There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:143:*Sheets: eleven. Dry. Not looked at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:153:She came across to him on her knees, with what remained of the blanket strip, and took his boot into her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:She bound the calf as she would have ruled a table. He supposed that she had watched a healer do it under a rock, and had kept it, as she kept everything; she was not gentle, and she was very exact, and when she tied it off the knot lay flat. She patted the leg once, absently, as she had patted a box on Brom's back. Then she went and lay down on her side against that box with her back to all of them, in Seln's coat, as she had lain on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:159:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:Brom had come in. He did not wait to be asked. He knelt down heavily beside the sleeping healer, box and all, and laid the bow in the grass with the bolt still on it, and held out both his hands into the light, palms up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:181:That was the camp. Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:183:He had expected questions, and he had been given a blanket, and a sixth of a loaf, and a knot that lay flat. He did not know yet which he would have found the easier to bear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:205:*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:245:He had thought her asleep. He had put the lantern out to save Karis's three fingers, and the stars had come down close over the grass; she lay on her back under two blankets, with her mended arm tied across her, and her face was a pale shape turned up to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:The mule shifted. Somewhere to the north Brom cleared his throat, and was quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:1:# Chapter 34 — Maps with Holes
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:27:He had been looking straight back up the lane. That was his place; the man on point looks behind him as often as before. He had seen the small one go down, and a grey animal step out between her and his knife, and beyond those the two women, and the box. Behind the box there had been a boy with a blade in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:Then there had been the lane, and the grass, and the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:55:Thirty-one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:71:He did not go to his blanket. He went along the line to the north, with the burnt strip on his right hand and then the unburnt grass, counting Brom's turfs as he came to them. They lay grass downward, each a cord from the last, and the frost had made them grey. At the third he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:79:A case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:There had been eight sheets. They were fair copies, on paper backed with linen, and they were of the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:89:And on some of the sheets, out in the white, there had been a round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:91:It was a small exact circle, ruled with a compass. He remembered the prick of the point at its middle, where the paper had been dented. There was nothing inside it at all; a dashed road came up to one and went about it and on. At the foot of each sheet there had been a little table of signs, a ford, a hold, a hill, and no sign among them for a round with nothing in it. On one sheet there were two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:95:He had taken those rounds for what a junior takes everything for, which is a thing that he has not been told. He had supposed a post, or a store, with a guard on it. It had never once entered his head that a place might be left off a map because of what the ground itself did there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:97:Seln stayed on his heels by the iron a long while, and went over what he had, strictly, as he would have gone over it for somebody else's signature. He had eight sheets, seen once, by a boy who was counting. He had rounds, ruled, with a road going about one of them. He had a stake under his eyes, of the pattern that is signed for, driven on the edge of a circle that a researcher had paced with her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:99:He did not have the leaf. There would be one. A sheet with a hole in it has a page somewhere that says what the hole is for; that was how every office that he had ever served drew breath. He had never seen it. He did not know what room it lay in, or under what heading, or whose hand had ruled those rounds. He knew the weight of a tin and the number of its sheets, and he knew that he would be asked for more than that before the sun was high, by a small person with a pencil and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:He could not tell, from memory, where on those sheets a round had fallen. He had the look of one: a water crossed, and dashes running on east of it, and then the white place. To say more than that he needed a ford drawn by somebody who had put a wheel through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:109:"I should like the carter's map," said Seln. "And the charcoal, if it came east in that box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:149:He had not expected it. He had thought that she would take her two hours first; but she was looking at the calf, where a strip of grey blanket had gone dark in the night, and she held out her hand for it as for a tool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:He had been at the stake since first light, by himself, with a calfskin and a stick of charcoal and a sheet of Karis's clean paper. Cael had watched him from the camp: a narrow dark figure three cords up the line, sitting on its heels, that drew a little and then sat for a long while with its hands hanging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:They went at Lira's pace. She walked it flat, in the great boot, with her right hand on Seln's sleeve and her left tied across her breast; and Cael came behind on a leg that gave him eleven strides and then shut like a fist, as it had done once before, on a green rock in a river. He stood until it let go. It was a count that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:191:[[TIER-B: Oryn sitting against the stake on the 97th, summing her mendings, "Three in two days… That's a route." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:195:"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:207:Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:209:"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:211:He took up the sheet of paper. It was thin stuff, the kind that Karis had rubbed stones with, and when he laid it over the hide the carter's brown ink showed through it faintly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:"I have traced three things from his map, and no more. Here is the first water; those are his three strokes. Here is his square with a dot, which is the hold we slept at. And here is his day's measure, a tick to a day, so that what I draw shall be on his scale and not on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:215:He lifted the sheet away and laid it on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:217:"The rest I have put in from memory, with the paper off the skin, so that I could not be led. On the sheets that I counted, a road went out from the Line in dashes. It crossed a water. It ran on east. And at a certain distance past the crossing, the dashes went round a white place, ruled with a compass, that had nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:231:"Five years ago by the stake, maybe six. They paced the circles and stamped the stakes and drew the maps with holes where the circles are. That's not ignorance. Ignorance doesn't stamp iron."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:235:"There was a sheet with two. I cannot tell you where the second lay, or the third, if there was a third. I counted corners." His eyes went to the two small rings on the hide beside which Karis had written *guess*, and came away. "I shall not promote a guess of hers on a memory of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:257:"I carried the maps. I never saw the file. I'm not going to draw you one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:267:Cael read them twice. They were the whole of it, and there was not a verb in them that reached past what six people had seen with their eyes. A map had a blank where this place was. A bar of iron had a stamp. And somewhere there was a page, which none of them would ever read, in a room that the one person who might have known could not name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:269:He had been ruling a line since a wash, through a query and a posting and a stake. He had it now with a fourth point on it, and for the first time he could see which way along it he was looking; and it ran west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:271:He took the cream sheet out of Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:It had ridden there since the ninety-first morning, folded once, between a letter in a narrow upright hand and a leaf out of a ledger. He opened it on his knee, and they heard it again as Pike had given it to a meadow: the recovery, and the instruments, and the rise where a road left its line. A hundred marks, in words, and again in a bracket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:281:"A person who writes a sum twice, so that it cannot be altered, does not forget where he has put a brass level." Seln held out his hand for the sheet, and when he had it he held it up unread to the afternoon, as he had held it to a lamp, with the light behind. "I said in that inn that it had been written by somebody who learned to write inside. I said that it was not a reason against going. I should have gone on, and asked what it was a reason for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:285:"It is a question. It is put to this hillside, and the hillside cannot write. So it is put to whoever will walk here for a hundred marks; and there is a clerk at a landing, with ink on his cuffs, who has been told to see what is brought down and send word of it west by the next boat." He lowered the paper. "The word that he sends does not need to have a brass level in it. It needs only to say what became of the crew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He had one other mark on that slope. North of the lane a rut came down from under the floor's north-western lip, knee-deep and a stride across, where the rain off the rim had cut itself a road. He had marked the head of it from the ninth cord, and Seln had crossed it on both his rounds in the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:They did not hurry, and it was that which he liked least. They spread out among the eleven stones as a crew spreads out in its own yard at the end of a day. One sat and scratched. The pale one stood at the middle of the round, by the place where a cord had ended, with its nose down on the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:Behind him the slope fell away to the east, and the line on that side was nearer by twenty-six yards. It was also the hounds' own door. There were three runs across it and not a stride of it that any of the six had walked; the grass stood hip-high the whole way down, the ground was steep, and in a quarter of an hour it would be dark. If they came out alive on that side, they would be half a circle of open country from a mule. The west was a lane trodden flat by their own feet, with a stone at the end of it that five of them could find with their eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:"There's a bow on that mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:"I looked at it at noon and left it lying. I'd come to find out about my hands, and I didn't want anything in them." Her mouth went hard. "That was a healer's reason. Six bolts, in a sleeve on the stock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:53:Nobody asked him why. He saw Seln take up the strap of the locked case by two holes, as he had taken it up at a ford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:65:"Round by the south," he said. "On the turf, close in under the lip, and nobody runs. Lira's first, with the knife. Then Karis, and Oryn. I'm behind you. Brom's last, and he's on the floor side." He looked at the hundredweight of paper on Brom's back. "Leave the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:71:"It's eleven sheets and her whole afternoon. It's the only thing we're bringing down off this hill." Brom shifted the plaited strap an inch across his chest, and took the bill in both hands like a quarterstaff, with the hook uppermost. "It weighs what it weighed at noon. I tried it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:73:Karis did not thank him. She put one small hand flat against the box as she went by, as a person touches a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:159:He was coming at the best pace that a man can make under a box, and it would not be enough, since there were twelve yards of turf between Brom's pole and Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:257:Seln drew a bolt from the sleeve on the stock, and laid it in the groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:319:The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held both hands away from himself for a breath, as a man holds them who has picked up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:47:"I have set one point since the mountain, on a lamp-wick. It ought to be very nearly the whole." Her voice was small and dry. "Nine, as before, and the eight joins. I could hold them for a count of six and have something over. I was told not to spend it to the last copper where she was not, and she will be there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:He could see the stone. It was a paler thing at the lane's end, the size of a loaf. The mule stood beyond it with her long ears forward, hobbled, and in no fear at all; nothing in that grass wanted anything that she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:101:Cael was four strides behind the box on Brom's back, and ten from Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:That left one. The healer knew nothing of him, and she spoke to every carter on seven roads. She had her back to him. A big man and a hundredweight of box stood between her and the place where he was, and her eyes were on Karis. She would see where he ended. She could not see where he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:121:The hiss of the seed-heads against his coat stopped. That was how it came: the world went on, and he went out of it. His boots came down on trodden grass and gave him nothing back; he could not hear his own breath. He went wide of the lane on the south side, through stems that stood to his hip and bent for him and did not rustle, past the box on Brom's back and Brom's left shoulder. Brom's eye came round and slid over the place where he was, as an eye slides over one fence-post among forty, and then came back to it, since Brom knew. He passed Lira on her left hand, a yard out. Her head turned. She was looking at grass that leaned with nobody in it, and he saw her mouth open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:149:He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:209:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:281:There was grass under him that was only grass, and a mule's shape, and a big man down on one knee a yard in front of him with a bow-stock at his cheek. Brom's eye was not on him. It was on something past his hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:285:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. One of them had an arm bitten through and an ankle gone for the third time, and one could not speak. One was looking at the fingers of both his hands by starlight. He himself had a calf laid open, and a forearm in which the feeling was going out like a tide, and nine things under his hand again that had not yet stopped shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:309:There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.

