editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:"It's a dog," said Brom. He had both hands tucked under his arms, like a man kept in from a harvest. "It weighs what a calf weighs. I could have it under one arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:13:"You'd be shutting eight new fingers on a hind leg. Stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:43:Once Brom got as far as "When you went up that hill—" and stopped, and looked at his new fingers, and asked whether the leg would walk tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Throat and fingers mended at noon; nothing came. My calf read and not mended: the fourth time she's found nothing. The dead one drawn off south. The bolt cut out: five in the sleeve. Bread in sixths, by Brom, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:63:That night, the ninety-seventh, Lira lay on her back with one arm tied across her chest and found that she could not get comfortable round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:65:It did not hurt. That was the stupid part. She had been bitten to the bone at dusk and by midnight she had a forearm with eight small pink marks on it, as neat as stitching, and when she moved the fingers they moved. It was a perfectly good arm. It was not hers yet. It belonged to a square woman who was asleep six feet away with her mouth open, and it would go on belonging to her until there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:She turned her head. The boy was sitting up, a little way off, with his dead arm in his lap and his face to the east. He was not on watch, and he had not lain down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She left the ankle alone. She had asked a healer once, from the back of a mule, and had been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed; and last night that same healer had watched her not ask and had answered regardless. That made twice told. She would not make it twice asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:85:She had been looking at Karis. There was a dog on Karis, and Seln's knife was coming up too slowly, and the healer had gone rigid under her arm; and on her left hand, where there was nobody, the grass had leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:105:So he had known on that floor, with her arm in its mouth, and he had held its head and let Brom shoot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:That left *since when*. She had the right to it. She could have had it out of him last night with one more word; he had been sitting there waiting for it with his breath held, and he would have told her everything, badly, in the wrong order, with his arm dead and hers tied, because she had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:129:The second time she had two fingers of her right hand on her own left wrist, and her lips moving. He saw them stop as her boot came down. She stood so for a breath, and came out, and they began again before her heel had left the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:She led the animal up until its forefeet were against the stone, and laid her whole hand on the warm neck under the mane, and stood with her head bent, reading whatever it is that a healer reads in a beast. Then, keeping her hand where it was, she stepped over, so that she stood inside with her arm stretched back across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:165:It was the arm that she could not get used to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:She had not known how much of walking was done with the hands. Her balance was all on one side. When the wind pushed her she put out the right and nothing answered it from the left; when she stepped over a rut she felt the tied arm start, under the grey wool, and stop against the knot. It did that forty times in the first hour. It was like walking beside a dog that keeps going for the same gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:It was not a decision. Lira had caught that small person off a ladder, and out of a chair, and once by the collar on the edge of a wash; her left hand knew the way. It was out of the sling to the wrist before any part of her had been consulted. She felt the wool go slack on her neck and the fingers open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:191:She stopped it. She did not know afterward how. It was like stopping dead off a full run at the lip of a gravel bar: there was all that going in her, and then there were two feet. She shut the hand and jammed the elbow back against her ribs, and she turned her right shoulder in, hard, the wrong way round, and took Karis on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:193:It was too slow, and it was too clumsy. A right arm is not where a left arm would have been. Karis struck it and slid, and went down on one knee in the rut with a jolt that brought a noise out of her throat, a croak, the first sound that she had made since the lane; and the notebook flew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:197:Karis looked up at her. She looked at the sling, and at the hand that had gone back into it, and at Lira's face. Then she got up by herself, brushing her knee, and found her pencil, and wrote something quite short and held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:209:She walked on, flat. The arm lay where it had been put. It had started again before she had gone a hundred yards, at a gust, and she felt it reach the knot and stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:301:Lira went in under the lintel on the flat of her foot. On his platform by the western gate, across the whole width of the yard, a lean man stood up with a bow in his hand and looked at how she walked, and at what was tied across her; and he did not lift two fingers. He took his cap off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:309:He got it over his head with one arm and the other, which had come back to him on the road as far as the wrist. He stood by the well's coping in the last of the sun with five bands of linen round him that he had hated on the eighty-sixth day, and measured on the eighty-seventh, and since a certain evening had not once had the leisure to think about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:315:She did not put her hands round him to count. She had tried that. She set her thumb on the lowest of the three, where the first small sound had been, and pressed, as she had pressed on Lira's shin under a rock; and she watched his face while she did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:327:He put his boot up on the coping. She unwound her own linen and turned the calf to the light. There was a seam down it, pale and dry, a hand long, lying over two older marks; she ran her thumb the length of it and did not shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:329:"That opened three nights back. It's closed. Four days, counting the one it went on." She let his leg go. "I've a man in this hold who cut his shin on a scythe at the start of the harvest and it's weeping yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:363:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:367:They got up, the five of them, and went back to whatever they had been doing; nobody on that bench had ever argued with her about a day. Cael stood by the well with his shirt in his hand and the cold on his ribs, and watched five people give up a morning to him that not one of them knew they were giving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:373:"I've climbed that with a foot on a stave," she said, when he came over. "I'm working out how you do it with an arm that's not allowed to hold." She kept her eyes on the rungs. "Don't help. I'd like to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:375:She went up it sideways, in the end, with her right hand and both knees, and her left elbow tight against her ribs; and at the top she sat down on the loft's edge with her legs hanging, breathing hard, and looked at the sling as a person looks at a dog that has stayed when it was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:7:For the first dozen there were only her hands. They were warm, and broad, and they did not move. He could feel the short nails of the left one against the back of his skull, and the heel of the right resting on bone. He could feel her pulse in it, which was slow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:He felt it leave her right hand at about the thirtieth breath. It was not warmth, and it was not a touch; it was nearer to what a person feels when somebody else's attention comes to rest on the back of his neck. It set out from his breastbone toward his feet. It went the whole length of him, without haste, as water goes when a sluice is lifted at the head of a dry channel; and when it had reached as far as there was to reach, it turned and came back up the other side, and he felt it arrive under the nape of his neck, in her left hand, where she was waiting for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:37:"There it is. I've got the moving. I had that much at a ford with two fingers." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "Now I go and look for what it's moving in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:59:"The small one runs hot. I felt the heat of hers before I'd found her wrist, on that mountain, and she was empty as a dry well at the time. That's an Ember. The bed's narrow and it's warm to the hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:His right palm had grown warm on the plank. He left it where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:65:She was silent for several breaths. He felt the thing that she was doing go more slowly along his left side, where three ribs had been, and then down the leg that a hound had opened, and stop there, and go on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:87:It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:111:He turned that over, slowly, at a man asleep's pace. She had watched him go up a hill with fire in his fist, in a place where she could not count to four, and she had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm. She had read his leg afterward and found nothing, and said so, and bound it in cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:119:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was not weaker. It had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge. She looked for it under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:149:He knew it before it had finished coming. He had felt it nine times. The first four he had hardly known that he was there for; after that he had learned to be present. It had always come in the thick of a blow, or at the end of a bout, with his arm ringing. It had never before come into a quiet room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:173:It was a small thing to do. He had not known how small. He did not so much as shift a finger on the plank. He opened the one door in the house that had been shut, and something came through it at her pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:192:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:222:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:3:There was a finger of oil in Karis's lantern, and he meant to have said all of it before that was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:Lira had the sack nearest the light, with her right hand laid over the sling. Brom sat on the boards with his knees up and his two hands open on them, pale across the pads of the fingers. Karis had her notebook, and a throat the colour of an old apple; she had spoken twice that day, a word at a time, in a voice like a rasp on wood. Seln had placed himself by the ladder's head, where he could hear the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:25:Karis lifted one finger from the notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:61:He heard how that sounded, in a loft, with a girl's arm tied up six feet from him; and he did not soften it, since that was the very thing that he had brought them up a ladder to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:"The second is that it isn't all mine to tell. I can't say what I am without saying that I've nine, and where a thing of that kind comes from; and then she is within one question of the one that's sealed. She talks to every carter on seven roads. She would never mean any harm by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:"Then it's wrong," he said. "I'd not stop you; I'd not know how, and it isn't mine to. But somebody ought to say it who's glad of what you carry, and I am. I was glad of it on a ridge and at a ford, and three nights back, for her arm." He jerked his chin at the sling. "It's wrong to do that to a person who climbed a mountain for five strangers on a carter's word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:"You could have done it and told us after," she said. "You could have told nobody at all. You carried it up a ladder and let four people call it what it is." She touched the top of his head once, with two fingers, as she might have touched a card for luck. "It's still wrong. Go and write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:239:The healer was washing. She had a basin on the bench and her sleeves pushed above the elbow, and she did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:245:The plank was cold under his shoulders and very clean, and there was a folded sack for his head. He lay and looked at rafters with barley dust on them, and listened to her drying her hands, a finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:261:Two fingers came down on the inside of his right wrist, and two more below the hinge of his jaw, and they were warm from the basin. Her lips moved. He did not count with her this time. He lay under it and felt the count come to five, and to six, where it had stopped on a leg at a stone; and it went on to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:265:She said it without any disappointment, as she would have said that a door was shut. It was the fifth time. She took her fingers away and shook both hands out from the wrist, twice, hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:273:Her left hand slid in under the back of his neck, broad and warm, and took the weight of his head. Her right came down flat on his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:9:The camp was quiet behind him. He listened to it as he listened to any room that he had his back to, and sorted what he heard: a pencil, and then no pencil; a big man washing his hands without a sound; and the healer's breathing, which was slow and went all the way down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:83:It was a tin, as long as his forearm, with a cap at one end and a lead tag crimped through the hasp. He had it under his arm. He was young, in the memory, with a satchel and one key, and he was carrying the tin from one room to another; that was the whole of the errand. A junior was permitted to break the tag in the second room, before a senior, and to draw out what was in the tin and count it against a docket. He was to count. He was not to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:121:She took Karis first. Two fingers went to the inside of a thin wrist, and the other hand lay along the front of the throat, lightly, where there was a bruise the shape of two fists. Her lips went to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:133:Brom was beside her already, on his knees, with the rags unwound. The cuts had dried black across eight finger-pads, and the two deep ones stood a little open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:[[TIER-B: Oryn mending Brom's string-cut fingers at noon on the 97th, "String… That's two." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:145:She sat back, and Cael saw what the pair of them had cost her. She had been grey when she woke, and she was grey again. A Tide on a road had two in her, and she had spent the whole of yesterday's on one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:149:He had not expected it. He had thought that she would take her two hours first; but she was looking at the calf, where a strip of grey blanket had gone dark in the night, and she held out her hand for it as for a tool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:171:Her eyes came up to his, level and tired and a little hungry, as they had come up once over three broken ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:173:"It's closing. I can see it closing. It opened last night and it's half shut, with nothing doing it, the same as those ribs." She reached for clean linen. "You get what a carter gets. Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:He had been at the stake since first light, by himself, with a calfskin and a stick of charcoal and a sheet of Karis's clean paper. Cael had watched him from the camp: a narrow dark figure three cords up the line, sitting on its heels, that drew a little and then sat for a long while with its hands hanging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:183:"I have something to show," he said. "I should like it shown where the iron is. It is three cords, and she may take my arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost. He had Lira's arm in it, and a throat, and eight fingers; he had his own leg, and a number in a book. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and the stop had taken three mendings in two days from seven gates that had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:207:Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:221:Seln laid the paper down on the calfskin again, and fitted the three wavy strokes to the three beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:301:"What does he write? The one-armed man. How many?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:Lira was on her back in the grass, and the healer knelt beside her. She had the stiff black linen off the arm already, a turn at a time, and laid in a heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:15:She put her hands round the arm, one above the place and one below, as she had put them round it at the head of a lane and had them come away empty. Her eyes shut. Her lips moved, and this time there was a count in them; he saw it go to four, and to five, and on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:She said it to the arm, on a breath, and that was all the notice that she gave it; she had waited thirty rounds to learn what a line did to her hands, and learned it at noon, and tonight she had no time to spare for having them back. She opened her eyes and looked up at five people, and spoke quickly, as she had spoken on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:25:"It can. The arm can't." Her mouth was hard. "So keep that bow up, and somebody look the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with eight fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"Lira's arm, and her ankle. Karis's throat. Brom's hands. Six bolts, at noon: three in the sleeve, one on the string, one a yard from that stone, and one gone. The box is whole, and there are eleven sheets in it and on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:41:"Mine's a calf, and an arm that's been struck. And what went up that hill with a light was me. I did it on purpose, to take them off the lane. I've no more to say about it tonight than that it was done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:45:"Is that calf worse than this arm?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:49:"Is anything you've got worse than this arm?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:55:She shut her eyes. Her hands settled, with the fingers overlapping, and her face went as quiet as a pond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:61:He did not watch the arm. He had seen what was to be seen of that at the head of a lane, and he found that he had no wish to learn how it looked when the linen was off. A healer on a mountain had grown a little older while he looked at her, over a shin. It was more than a little now. The sweat came out along her cropped hair in the first minutes and ran, though the frost was coming down, and by the middle of the hour the skin under her eyes had gone the colour of a bruise; her breath came slowly, at the pace that she walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:65:He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, by the light of a brass lamp, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it she had not expected, while a stranger's hands took six weeks out of her arm in an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left forearm went first. The ringing had gone out of the bone before he reached the stone, and something duller had come into its place, which spread; by the end of the first quarter he could not have told by feel where his fingers were. He set the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:73:Something in his right arm had begun to ask about the lantern's rim. *Edge?* It had asked that question of half the world for a season, patiently, and tonight for the first time it had been told yes; and now it asked like a child that has been given one sweet. He told it no, and it asked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:77:Under all of that the calf burned, slowly, in its own blood. He looked at none of it for long. He had opened a purse and counted out what was in it, and here was the reckoning, an item at a time, on his own body; and every item was smaller than the one that the healer had her hands on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:85:When her hands opened, she did not take them away at once. She turned the arm over in them, toward the light, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:97:Brom said something to the dark that was not a word. Cael found that the lantern was shaking, and that it was not his dead arm that shook it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:101:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:111:Nobody answered her, and she did not seem to want an answer. She held out her hand to the south, and a narrow dull knife came into it out of the dark, haft first. That one cut. She took a strip off the blanket a hand wide and as long as her two arms, and passed it under Lira's elbow and round her neck, and tied it short, so that the mended arm lay across the breast with its hand under the opposite collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:115:"One arm. From here to the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:121:"The ankle's a tape and a season. I've told you that before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:127:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything: the healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning, Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:She bound the calf as she would have ruled a table. He supposed that she had watched a healer do it under a rock, and had kept it, as she kept everything; she was not gentle, and she was very exact, and when she tied it off the knot lay flat. She patted the leg once, absently, as she had patted a box on Brom's back. Then she went and lay down on her side against that box with her back to all of them, in Seln's coat, as she had lain on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:Across the pads of four fingers on each there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled, and in two of them it had gone deep. They had bled down into his cuffs and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:181:That was the camp. Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:197:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: eight fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:221:*Compression: in that lock, late. That's the arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:245:He had thought her asleep. He had put the lantern out to save Karis's three fingers, and the stars had come down close over the grass; she lay on her back under two blankets, with her mended arm tied across her, and her face was a pale shape turned up to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:267:She turned her head a little, then, toward him, and he thought that she was going to say something else. She did not. She shut her eyes; and presently her breathing lengthened, and he knew by it that the arm had stopped hurting her enough to let her go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:269:He sat on beside her with a dead arm across his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:277:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:That was the price of the quietest thing he owned, with the width of a floor between: one head, once. He did not have to work out what the other eight would cost. Any one of them, used here, would bring nine animals to the place where everybody he had in the world was standing within reach of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:119:This one went down harder, for Brom had hit it as he would have hit a post, with both arms and the hundredweight on his back behind them. It lay a breath on the turf with its legs working. Then it too stood up, and shook, and went home along the lip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:125:"I dropped that first one hard enough to stun a calf. And this one walked up to the same place as if I'd never been born. It watched me do it. It was standing there on the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:He saw the foot go over. It went outward, past the place where an ankle stops, as it had gone on round stones at a river and under a plate of shale; and that made three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:189:The pale one took her by the left forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:It did not snap. It closed on the arm between the wrist and the elbow, as a door closes on a hand, and its jaws stayed shut. She was on her back, and he saw her do then what he had seen her do at a stone at noon: she gathered the whole of herself, heels and hips and shoulders, and asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:193:At noon it had bought her a step. It bought her a yard of floor now, sliding, with the animal's forefeet sliding after her and her arm in its mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:213:"Whatever else you do. If it shakes that arm there'll be nothing for me to mend."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:He was thirty inside his time. He came over the lip with a long thing under one arm, out of its cover, and no breath to speak with. He gave the bow to Brom, and stood holding his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:249:"There is a hook for the string," he said, when he could. "A healer does not draw that with her fingers. I did not stay to look for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:251:"Pannier," said Oryn. "Near side. It doesn't matter. He's the arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:253:Brom had never held one, as Cael saw from how he turned it over; but he had drawn carts and hauled rope all his life, and he found the iron stirrup at its nose, and put his boot in that, and bent and took the string in the fingers of both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:255:He straightened his back, and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch with a sound like a gate-latch. When he took his hands away there was a dark line across the pads of four fingers, and he wiped them down his breeches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:291:Brom carried her the four strides, on one arm, with the bow in the other hand, and Oryn was on her knees in the grass before Lira's back had touched it. Both her hands went round the arm, above the place and below it, as they had gone round a shin on a mountain, and her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:293:[[TIER-B: Oryn at the rim, hands on Lira's bitten arm, "Nothing… Nothing in my hands." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:295:She took her hands away as though the arm had burnt them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:297:Then she was a different person, and one that he had not met. She had a roll of linen out of her coat, the stiff kind that smelled of a pine wood, and her right knee on the inside of Lira's arm above the elbow, with all her weight on the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:299:There was a good deal of blood, and it was black in that light. The teeth had met through the arm; he knew what that was, from the inside of his own calf, and he knew that his calf had been the smaller matter. He had seen her hurt in a dozen ways since a lane in Ardenmere, and he had never before seen so much of her on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:311:"I heard it too. It'll carry you flat. Put the whole sole down, every step, like a duck." Oryn got her own shoulder under Lira's right arm, and stood up with her. She was the shorter by half a head, and she took the weight as she would have taken a pannier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held both hands away from himself for a breath, as a man holds them who has picked up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:157:The landing locked. *One*, and everything that he owned went dumb; there was a boy standing in a field with a knife, who could not have shifted a foot to save his life. He had priced that count before he spent it. The thrown one was on the south side, finding its legs, and the rest were strung along sixty yards of lane. The nearest of them was a good deal more than one count from his ankles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:165:He drew breath for Pressure. It was a deeper breath than a healer's tape allowed, and his left side told him so from the breastbone to the spine. It gathered under his ribs for the better part of that breath, as it had always gathered, slowly, where anything alive might feel it coming. There was no opponent to send it through. He sent it straight down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:169:Ember was next. He gave it the one place where he could carry it, which was the tip of Lira's knife; and for the first time since the Line he did not stop at the setting. It lit. A seed of yellow stood on the steel, and then a flame as long as his thumb, burning on nothing, at the end of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:177:Something bright and quick stirred in his right arm and asked what it had asked of every sill and rim and arris since the day it came to him. *Edge?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:*Yes*, he told that one too. He had never said so before. Along the knife's cutting side a line of white stood out a hair clear of the steel, as a tall exact person had once made one stand on a lath by saying that it did; and it rang. There was nothing for it to cut. It sang all the same, thin and high, like a wet finger going round the rim of a glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:187:They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:213:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:231:Two. He made five people out of two voices, running. Seln would not have called with Karis still on the wrong side of that stone, and Karis could not call. Lira had a healer under her arm. Brom he had to take on trust; Brom would be the last of them over, and would not spend breath on a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:249:He could not move his feet. He could not have moved a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:253:It took it late. For the first beat there was nothing between him and a hound's whole weight but the left forearm that he had thrown across his body, and he felt the bone of that arm ring from the wrist to the elbow like a struck rail. Then the thing that a fighter had once put through him, and left with him, came up through his frame and did what it was for. The rest of the blow went through his shoulder and his spine and his locked knees, and down into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:261:It was two hundred yards to the stone, downhill. He ran it on a calf that was open, and a side that had been told *there* too many times to go on saying it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:263:He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:265:They were behind him. He could hear them come out of the rut, one after another, with a scrabbling of feet on its lip, and then the rushing in the grass. One sound was nearer than the rest, and gaining, and that was a thing with long legs, built for this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:She was on her knees a stride from the stone, with Seln's hands under her arms to keep her upright. She had no voice to answer him with. Her two hands were out in front of her, shaking, and he saw the right one make the smallest movement that it had ever made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:They split on it. The foremost flung left and the next flung right, and the rest went after one or the other, as thirty head of horn had gone on a mountain. They ran along the inside of that wall to its two ends. Then they were over the line, somewhere in the dark beyond the light, to the north and to the south; and the last that he knew of any of them was a rushing in the grass that went away along the line's outside in both directions, and thinned, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. One of them had an arm bitten through and an ankle gone for the third time, and one could not speak. One was looking at the fingers of both his hands by starlight. He himself had a calf laid open, and a forearm in which the feeling was going out like a tide, and nine things under his hand again that had not yet stopped shaking.

