editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:37:"There it is. I've got the moving. I had that much at a ford with two fingers." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "Now I go and look for what it's moving in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:49:He had refused the nine at every stride of a road, and he knew that work as he knew the weight of his own boots. He was not to refuse them now; a refusal was a holding, and she had said that she would know. He was not to let them answer either. He had to lie as a house lies when somebody goes through it with a lamp, with every door standing as it happened to stand, and he found that he had never in his life left a door alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:57:*Eighty-four.* It was passing his feet when she said it, and something in his feet turned over like a sleeper who has heard his name. It was Wind. He did not tell it no, and he did not let it rise. He let it turn over. It settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:69:He was at a hundred and eight. That was nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:87:It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:107:She had found it eight minutes ago. He had heard her find it: *there it is, I've got the moving.* She had been walking up and down the length of something for twelve minutes that was like nothing she had ever put her hands in. And her voice was the voice in which she had told an ox-driver that four small bones were broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:119:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was not weaker. It had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge. She looked for it under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:137:He was at two hundred and twenty-eight. That left one minute of her twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:And along the slow current that she kept, the one that set out from her right hand and came home to her left, he could feel a shape. It was not his. It was the way she went: the keeping of time, and the feeling ahead for a bank, and the patience of it, which would walk the length of a person eight times sooner than guess. It was the thing that she read with. She had laid the whole of it open on him for twenty minutes, in earnest, at a cost, and she was pressing it against him now harder than she had pressed it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:173:It was a small thing to do. He had not known how small. He did not so much as shift a finger on the plank. He opened the one door in the house that had been shut, and something came through it at her pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:175:The place behind his breastbone took on weight. It filled slowly at first and then all the way to the brim, like a cup under a careful hand; and he lay under a healer's palm and kept his eyes open through all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:222:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:240:He had not known that it would be so hard to say. It was only eight words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:"It's a dog," said Brom. He had both hands tucked under his arms, like a man kept in from a harvest. "It weighs what a calf weighs. I could have it under one arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:13:"You'd be shutting eight new fingers on a hind leg. Stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:43:Once Brom got as far as "When you went up that hill—" and stopped, and looked at his new fingers, and asked whether the leg would walk tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Throat and fingers mended at noon; nothing came. My calf read and not mended: the fourth time she's found nothing. The dead one drawn off south. The bolt cut out: five in the sleeve. Bread in sixths, by Brom, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:65:It did not hurt. That was the stupid part. She had been bitten to the bone at dusk and by midnight she had a forearm with eight small pink marks on it, as neat as stitching, and when she moved the fingers they moved. It was a perfectly good arm. It was not hers yet. It belonged to a square woman who was asleep six feet away with her mouth open, and it would go on belonging to her until there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:119:Cael had the last watch, on the ninety-eighth morning, and so he was the only one who saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:129:The second time she had two fingers of her right hand on her own left wrist, and her lips moving. He saw them stop as her boot came down. She stood so for a breath, and came out, and they began again before her heel had left the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:147:He looked at the pencil. It was a finding. She had made it eight times over, by a method, and it was about the line; Karis would have given a week's paper for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:155:They walked west on the ninety-eighth, and Lira went the whole way on the flat of her foot, since she had been told to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:It was not a decision. Lira had caught that small person off a ladder, and out of a chair, and once by the collar on the edge of a wash; her left hand knew the way. It was out of the sling to the wrist before any part of her had been consulted. She felt the wool go slack on her neck and the fingers open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:233:They all looked at it. In ninety-eight days of road nobody had seen such a thing come off one of their fires.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:243:*Finding. A method correctly dictated for ninety-eight days and never once followed by its author. Tested: one cake. I am entering it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:She would lay both hands on him in the morning, and all that she was, for as long as it took. She would do it in earnest, since she did nothing in any other way. He had felt that ninth thing come to him at the end of a bout with a friend who had asked only to be shown straight; he knew, as well as he knew anything about himself, what her morning was likely to be made of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:301:Lira went in under the lintel on the flat of her foot. On his platform by the western gate, across the whole width of the yard, a lean man stood up with a bow in his hand and looked at how she walked, and at what was tied across her; and he did not lift two fingers. He took his cap off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:309:He got it over his head with one arm and the other, which had come back to him on the road as far as the wrist. He stood by the well's coping in the last of the sun with five bands of linen round him that he had hated on the eighty-sixth day, and measured on the eighty-seventh, and since a certain evening had not once had the leisure to think about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:313:She took the first band at his breastbone, where its end was, and drew it off round his side to the spine in one pull, as she had laid it on. It came with a sound like a page tearing. The other four followed. He had expected to feel something give, and he felt only cold air on a place that had not had any for eighteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:337:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:363:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:3:There was a finger of oil in Karis's lantern, and he meant to have said all of it before that was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:Lira had the sack nearest the light, with her right hand laid over the sling. Brom sat on the boards with his knees up and his two hands open on them, pale across the pads of the fingers. Karis had her notebook, and a throat the colour of an old apple; she had spoken twice that day, a word at a time, in a voice like a rasp on wood. Seln had placed himself by the ladder's head, where he could hear the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:"She called it that at Oxhollow. Both hands, and all of her, for minutes, with the one under her hands lying still and whole. You were all in that yard, and I saw four people hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:25:Karis lifted one finger from the notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:It was a single leaf, and she had been two years in the making of it. It had a curve on it, for how quickly each of the nine had come, and a column for what each had cost him afterward; and at its head, in her small exact hand, stood three words with a rule under every one. She had never in his hearing called it more than a model. He had its three words by heart, and so had the others, since she had read them out on the night that the eighth was priced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:He had not shifted from his place on the boards. He was looking at his own open hands, where a healer had drawn eight new lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:189:"Then enter it tonight," said Seln. "By your own rule: before, and at its full weight. An entry made tomorrow will have learned how the morning went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:"You could have done it and told us after," she said. "You could have told nobody at all. You carried it up a ladder and let four people call it what it is." She touched the top of his head once, with two fingers, as she might have touched a card for luck. "It's still wrong. Go and write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:*Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:245:The plank was cold under his shoulders and very clean, and there was a folded sack for his head. He lay and looked at rafters with barley dust on them, and listened to her drying her hands, a finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:261:Two fingers came down on the inside of his right wrist, and two more below the hinge of his jaw, and they were warm from the basin. Her lips moved. He did not count with her this time. He lay under it and felt the count come to five, and to six, where it had stopped on a leg at a stone; and it went on to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:265:She said it without any disappointment, as she would have said that a door was shut. It was the fifth time. She took her fingers away and shook both hands out from the wrist, twice, hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:273:Her left hand slid in under the back of his neck, broad and warm, and took the weight of his head. Her right came down flat on his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:3:There were two hundred and seven yards of lane and eight hounds, and he had both figures by heart before Brom had the string back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held both hands away from himself for a breath, as a man holds them who has picked up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:He looked back at the third stride. The eight were coming down off the lip, one at a time, without haste, and going into the standing grass on either side of the lane. After that there was only grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:47:"I have set one point since the mountain, on a lamp-wick. It ought to be very nearly the whole." Her voice was small and dry. "Nine, as before, and the eight joins. I could hold them for a count of six and have something over. I was told not to spend it to the last copper where she was not, and she will be there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:77:At a hundred Lira's bad foot dragged, and she made no sound; he knew it only from how the healer's shoulder dropped to take her. At ninety Brom said, without turning, that there was something in the lane behind them, and Cael looked. One of the eight had come out onto the trodden grass thirty yards back, and was sitting there with its tongue out. It got up when they had gone on ten strides, and came after, and sat again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:79:"Eighty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:97:Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:107:He could let Wind have him. It would crack like a whip over the lane and set him down beside her with his feet locked for a count, and it would bring eight animals to the ground where Karis was lying, with him standing in the middle of them unable to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:There was a third thing, and it was already lifting. It had lifted at sunset behind the other eight, as it lifted at every dusk, with the one offer that it ever made. It did not put anything into the air. It was made to take something out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:That left one. The healer knew nothing of him, and she spoke to every carter on seven roads. She had her back to him. A big man and a hundredweight of box stood between her and the place where he was, and her eyes were on Karis. She would see where he ended. She could not see where he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:123:The healer was on Lira's other side, a head lower, with the whole of a girl's weight on her shoulder, and she did not turn at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He had its weight. He had been carrying a pace and a half of the world since the far lip, and he knew where every pound of that long body sat and which way it leaned. He took it by the loose skin behind the skull and by the root of the tail. He did not pull it off her, since a thing that is pulled bites down. He lifted the hind end as high as his chest and turned his whole back into it, as he would have thrown a sack onto a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:175:Eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:*Yes*, he told that one too. He had never said so before. Along the knife's cutting side a line of white stood out a hair clear of the steel, as a tall exact person had once made one stand on a lath by saying that it did; and it rang. There was nothing for it to cut. It sang all the same, thin and high, like a wet finger going round the rim of a glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:187:They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:197:The rut ran up the swell toward the floor, a little south of east, and every stride that he took along it was a stride away from a lane with a lame girl on it. He put his back to five people and went up the hill the wrong way, with eight animals pouring into the channel behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:213:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:217:*Eight. Nine. Ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:247:He had priced this count too, and the price was a bad one. The dark-backed one had not gone into the chaff. It had run the rut's rim. Whether a boy's two hands had taught it something in the lane he could not have said. It was the only one of the eight that had been on the right side of the channel when he left it, and it was three strides from him, and already in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:249:He could not move his feet. He could not have moved a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:253:It took it late. For the first beat there was nothing between him and a hound's whole weight but the left forearm that he had thrown across his body, and he felt the bone of that arm ring from the wrist to the elbow like a struck rail. Then the thing that a fighter had once put through him, and left with him, came up through his frame and did what it was for. The rest of the blow went through his shoulder and his spine and his locked knees, and down into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:303:Karis held it for a count of six. He counted it for her. At six the wall went down all at once, and she sat back against Seln's knees with her hands open in her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. One of them had an arm bitten through and an ankle gone for the third time, and one could not speak. One was looking at the fingers of both his hands by starlight. He himself had a calf laid open, and a forearm in which the feeling was going out like a tide, and nine things under his hand again that had not yet stopped shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with eight fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:55:She shut her eyes. Her hands settled, with the fingers overlapping, and her face went as quiet as a pond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left forearm went first. The ringing had gone out of the bone before he reached the stone, and something duller had come into its place, which spread; by the end of the first quarter he could not have told by feel where his fingers were. He set the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:85:When her hands opened, she did not take them away at once. She turned the arm over in them, toward the light, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:89:"Fingers," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:127:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:137:Karis had her notebook open on her knee, and she was writing with the pencil held in her fist. Her knuckles were skinned across both hands, where teeth had gone over them; she had looked at them once and left them. When she had three lines she turned the leaf to the lantern and to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:141:*Wall: nine points, eight joins, sixty yards. Count of six. Yours. Not empty: a tenth part left, perhaps. I have done as I was told.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:159:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:Across the pads of four fingers on each there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled, and in two of them it had gone deep. They had bled down into his cuffs and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:197:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: eight fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:245:He had thought her asleep. He had put the lantern out to save Karis's three fingers, and the stars had come down close over the grass; she lay on her back under two blankets, with her mended arm tied across her, and her face was a pale shape turned up to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:35:It had gone on for ten strides. Then there had been a boy with both hands on a hound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:37:Seln had done the sum himself, in the half-breath before. It was the first thing that a person of his trade did on entering any room, and he had done it without choosing to: who is here, and what can each of them see, and where will each of them be tomorrow. There were four who had known for years and had been tried. There was a healer, who was owed everything and knew nothing, and who walked seven roads. She had her back turned and a hundredweight of copied stone between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:There had been eight sheets. They were fair copies, on paper backed with linen, and they were of the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:93:He had counted eight, and said *eight*, and the senior had initialled the docket; and a young man with a satchel had gone down a stair thinking about his dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:97:Seln stayed on his heels by the iron a long while, and went over what he had, strictly, as he would have gone over it for somebody else's signature. He had eight sheets, seen once, by a boy who was counting. He had rounds, ruled, with a road going about one of them. He had a stake under his eyes, of the pattern that is signed for, driven on the edge of a circle that a researcher had paced with her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:99:He did not have the leaf. There would be one. A sheet with a hole in it has a page somewhere that says what the hole is for; that was how every office that he had ever served drew breath. He had never seen it. He did not know what room it lay in, or under what heading, or whose hand had ruled those rounds. He knew the weight of a tin and the number of its sheets, and he knew that he would be asked for more than that before the sun was high, by a small person with a pencil and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:121:She took Karis first. Two fingers went to the inside of a thin wrist, and the other hand lay along the front of the throat, lightly, where there was a bruise the shape of two fists. Her lips went to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:133:Brom was beside her already, on his knees, with the rags unwound. The cuts had dried black across eight finger-pads, and the two deep ones stood a little open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:[[TIER-B: Oryn mending Brom's string-cut fingers at noon on the 97th, "String… That's two." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:137:It was quicker than the throat. When she let go of him there were eight thin pale lines where the cuts had been, and he closed both fists, slowly, and opened them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost. He had Lira's arm in it, and a throat, and eight fingers; he had his own leg, and a number in a book. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and the stop had taken three mendings in two days from seven gates that had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:209:"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:285:"It is a question. It is put to this hillside, and the hillside cannot write. So it is put to whoever will walk here for a hundred marks; and there is a clerk at a landing, with ink on his cuffs, who has been told to see what is brought down and send word of it west by the next boat." He lowered the paper. "The word that he sends does not need to have a brass level in it. It needs only to say what became of the crew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:The sun had gone under the hills while Cael's book was shutting. He had seen the pale thing hold the light on the evening before, from three hundred yards, and had taken it for water; now he stood below its eastern lip, with the rolled edge at the height of his thigh, and saw what it did close to. The long shadows of the stones were gone, and the whole round of it lay level with his hip and gave back the sky, as milk in a pan gives back a window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:65:"Round by the south," he said. "On the turf, close in under the lip, and nobody runs. Lira's first, with the knife. Then Karis, and Oryn. I'm behind you. Brom's last, and he's on the floor side." He looked at the hundredweight of paper on Brom's back. "Leave the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:71:"It's eleven sheets and her whole afternoon. It's the only thing we're bringing down off this hill." Brom shifted the plaited strap an inch across his chest, and took the bill in both hands like a quarterstaff, with the hook uppermost. "It weighs what it weighed at noon. I tried it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:77:It came as it had come beside a square of black turf: a pace and a half of ground on every side, and the weight of everything that stood on it. He had the trodden earth, and the hollow going back under the floor, and five people's boots; and something went out of him into the cold, as small as a breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:That was the price of the quietest thing he owned, with the width of a floor between: one head, once. He did not have to work out what the other eight would cost. Any one of them, used here, would bring nine animals to the place where everybody he had in the world was standing within reach of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:103:It ought to have had him. Cael saw that as it came, and saw why it had not. A hound springs off its hind feet. On that rim its hind feet had nothing square to push from; they were set on a thing as round as a log, with the edge falling away in front, and so it came over the lip reaching down, with all its weight on its forelegs and its jaw stretched out. The haft took it under the chin. Brom turned his hips, and it went past his knee and onto its side on the turf with a grunt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:117:The dark-backed one came next, forty yards farther round. It was the heaviest of the nine, and it came to the very same kind of place, at Brom's right hand, and stood, and brought its weight forward in the very same way; and Cael said "Now," and the haft was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:119:This one went down harder, for Brom had hit it as he would have hit a post, with both arms and the hundredweight on his back behind them. It lay a breath on the turf with its legs working. Then it too stood up, and shook, and went home along the lip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:137:He grounded the haft and walked on, a foot from the lip, with a hundredweight of copied stone on his shoulders. Cael looked at the broad back in front of the grey, and at the wrists, which were the colour of wrists. Brom had stood on a rock in a river once and been the loudest thing for half a mile, on purpose, for three breaths; he was doing a harder thing now, which was to be nobody, with a pole, in the right place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:143:Cael did not see them go. He had the whole of his attention in a pace and a half of turf, feeling for the next weight to come onto the rim above Brom, and he had let his eyes follow it down; and when he lifted them the file had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:177:The pair turned as she passed them. He saw both heads go round together, and both bodies after, and they left Karis as a dog leaves a bone for a thrown stick and went loping along the foot of the kerb under her heels. On the white the pale one had seen her too, and it swung in beside her on the inside, easily, with its head at the height of her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:205:Brom was a stride behind, and he brought the haft down across the pale one's skull with both hands, in a blow that would have broken a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:215:Cael came down on the animal with both knees, behind the skull, and put both hands on its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:He could have ended it in a breath, three ways, and he went through what came after as quickly as he would have gone through a bout. Whichever he chose would go out of him on that floor a great deal louder than a healer's count, and eight heads would come round. There were two hounds within a yard of Brom's legs already, and Karis and the healer stood at the lip with nothing in their hands. He could take one animal, perhaps two; he could not take eight off four people who were standing inside the length of a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:249:"There is a hook for the string," he said, when he could. "A healer does not draw that with her fingers. I did not stay to look for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:253:Brom had never held one, as Cael saw from how he turned it over; but he had drawn carts and hauled rope all his life, and he found the iron stirrup at its nose, and put his boot in that, and bent and took the string in the fingers of both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:255:He straightened his back, and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch with a sound like a gate-latch. When he took his hands away there was a dark line across the pads of four fingers, and he wiped them down his breeches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:277:The head under his hands stopped pulling. There was no struggle in it: it was a weight that had been leaning against him, and then it was only a weight, and he was pressing a dead thing to the floor with all his strength.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:279:"Eight," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:283:The jaws had not opened. Seln knelt, and put the flat of the narrow dull knife between the teeth at the hinge, and Cael took the upper jaw in both hands, and it was done as a trap is opened. Lira made one sound while they did it, and it was not loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:297:Then she was a different person, and one that he had not met. She had a roll of linen out of her coat, the stiff kind that smelled of a pine wood, and her right knee on the inside of Lira's arm above the elbow, with all her weight on the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:301:"You," said Oryn, to Karis. "Both hands. There, where my thumb is. Harder than that. You'll not hurt her worse than she's hurt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:303:Karis put both hands where the thumb was, and leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:311:"I heard it too. It'll carry you flat. Put the whole sole down, every step, like a duck." Oryn got her own shoulder under Lira's right arm, and stood up with her. She was the shorter by half a head, and she took the weight as she would have taken a pannier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:317:He looked back once. The white lay behind them, a hand higher than the turf that he stood on, and it had not dimmed. One shape was stretched on it by the lip, four strides in, pale on pale, and eight others stood or sat among the feet of the stones. None of them had come a yard nearer while a bow was drawn and a girl was bound; they had watched it as the pale one had watched Brom from the rim.
