   149	He knew it before it had finished coming. He had felt it nine times. The first four he had hardly known that he was there for; after that he had learned to be present. It had always come in the thick of a blow, or at the end of a bout, with his arm ringing. It had never before come into a quiet room.
   150	
   151	There was a place in him that stood empty. He had not known of it until it was offered something.
   152	
   153	And along the slow current that she kept, the one that set out from her right hand and came home to her left, he could feel a shape. It was not his. It was the way she went: the keeping of time, and the feeling ahead for a bank, and the patience of it, which would walk the length of a person eight times sooner than guess. It was the thing that she read with. She had laid the whole of it open on him for twenty minutes, in earnest, at a cost, and she was pressing it against him now harder than she had pressed it yet.
   154	
   155	It stood at his breastbone, under her palm, like a hand held out in the dark.
   156	
   157	He could stop it.
   158	
   159	That was the first thing that he was sure of. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for a hundred days, and he was better at that one act than at anything else he did. He had only to say no, at the first stride, as he had said it on a gravel bar. Her current would go by the empty place and find it shut, as it had found the ninth; she would come to the end of her twenty minutes and lift her hands and tell him that there was nothing, as before. He would get down off her table with what he had lain down on it with. Nothing would have been taken from anybody.
   160	
   161	He went through what came after that, as quickly as he had gone through it on a white floor with his hands on a skull.
   162	
   163	He would not have to tell her. There would be nothing to tell. He would walk out into the yard having wanted a thing with his whole heart, and having climbed a ladder to say so, and having written it on a leaf with the price beside it; and at the last breath he would have kept his hands clean, and the leaf would be a page about a boy who had thought of doing wrong and had not. Four people in a loft would be glad. He would let them be.
   164	
   165	And it would be a lie. He saw that with great plainness. He had not written that he would be good. He had written that he wanted it, and that he would pay. To shut that place now, and call the shutting a conscience, would be to keep the whole of the wanting and send back only the bill; and he would be the registry after all, which had never once owned to what it reached for.
   166	
   167	He did not tell himself that it was right. Four people had told him what it was, and he had agreed with every one of them.
   168	
   169	He looked at it squarely, with his neck loose and his eyes open on a rafter.
   170	
   171	*He reached. Directed. Open-eyed. Still.*
   172	
   173	It was a small thing to do. He had not known how small. He did not so much as shift a finger on the plank. He opened the one door in the house that had been shut, and something came through it at her pace.
   174	
   175	The place behind his breastbone took on weight. It filled slowly at first and then all the way to the brim, like a cup under a careful hand; and he lay under a healer's palm and kept his eyes open through all of it.
   176	
   177	The lines came after. They stood where they had always stood, plain, in the place that belonged to nothing else, and nobody in that room could see them but himself.
   178	
   179	```
   180	FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
   181	[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
   182	Tier equivalent: Iron.
   183	Note: current-perception component; architecture-reading component. Self and contact.
   184	Contact range.
   185	Acquisition: directed. Engagement: clinical — non-hostile.
   186	```
   187	
   188	Ten.
   189	
   190	He read it through, from the top, with her hand still on him.
   191	
   192	It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
   193	
   194	He lay and looked at that. He had wanted it with his whole heart in a loft, and had told four people why: that he might look. It sat under his breastbone now, as real as the other nine, and he did not try it. He did not know how. And he had a healer's hand on him still, and a debt to pay before he had any right to learn.
   195	
   196	He counted what was new in it, since he could not have helped counting.
   197	
   198	It was the first that had ever come to him lying down. It had come out of nobody's blow, in a room with a basin in it, from two hands that were trying to learn what he was made of. That was one.
   199	
   200	It was the first whose last line had a healer's word in it. He had seen *non-hostile* once before, at the end of a bout with a friend, and had not known what to do with it. He had never seen *clinical*. Whatever kept those lines had looked at what she did and had found the right name for it. That was two.
   201	
   202	There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
   203	
   204	---
   205	
   206	Her hands had gone still.
   207	
   208	They had not lifted. But the slow going-along had stopped, somewhere down by his knees, and it did not set out again. He heard her breath catch and hold.
   209	
   210	He looked up at her. He had not done that since she shut her eyes. Her face hung over him, upside down, grey, with the sweat standing on it and the cropped hair dark; and the crease had come between her brows, the one that she wore over a sum that had come out oddly.
   211	
   212	Her eyes opened.
   213	
   214	She was looking straight down into his. He had seen those eyes go over a yard of people in the time that it takes to count five. They were not going over anything now.
   215	
   216	"Something just changed," she said. "In you. While I was in there."
   217	
   218	"Yes."
   219	
   220	He had told her not one true thing about himself since a hollow of shale, beyond what she had asked; and he gave her that word as he would have handed her the first coin of a debt.
   221	
   222	She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
   223	
   224	He watched her do what she did at a bench. She did not guess. She went over what her hands had found, strictly, and came to the one thing that a healer must ask first.
   225	
   226	"Did I do that?"
   227	
   228	There was no fright in it. It was the question that she would have put if a patient had gone white under her on the ash at Oxhollow.
   229	
   230	He could have let her think so. He saw it open in front of him, as easy as a gate: it would have been a healer's hands doing something that they had never done before, to a boy that they could not read, and she would have carried it north with her and turned it over at every hold for a year.
   231	
   232	"No. I did."
   233	
   234	The crease deepened. She waited. She was a person who knew how to let somebody come to the end of his own load.
   235	
   236	He had a hundred days of road behind him in which he had been asked what he was by nobody. He had thought, on a shelf above a valley, that this was freedom, and had written it down. He lay now under the hands of the one person past the Line who had tried to find out without a seal, and who had just paid for the trying with something that he did not yet know the size of; and he had a leaf in a loft that said what he owed.
   237	
   238	"I need to tell you what I am."
   239	
   240	He had not known that it would be so hard to say. It was only eight words.
   241	
   242	Oryn looked at him for as long as it takes to draw one breath, and he could not tell what was in her face. It was not fear. It had never been that. Then the corner of her mouth went down, a very little, as it went when a patient on her table began to sit up before she had done.
   243	
   244	"Then lie still while you do it. You've been still for twenty minutes. I want to see if you can manage twenty-one."
   245	

