 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:*Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:227:"I have folded a great many letters," said Seln. He had flour to the wrists, and his face was as mild as milk. "I had not understood that it was a disadvantage."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:337:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:Cael brought it down with his right hand. The left would not close properly on the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:101:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:3:The ferryman would not take silver for his rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Sixty yards of four-strand. I laid that rope up myself, in the cold term, with him turning the hook." He jerked his chin at his boy. Then he looked at Karis, who had the purse out on her knee, and his long face soured further. "Put that away. What'd I do with it? There's not a rope-walk nearer than the turf fire, and I can't draw a wagon out of a river with marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:"A rope. When you've one. I'll not put a figure on it, for a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Karis wrote it down as he had said it. *Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman. No price.* She put the purse away uncounted, and Cael thought that it was the first debt she had ever entered without a number beside it, and that she did not like the look of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:A hundred marks was more than everything that the board had paid them in ninety days, the mountain included. It was to walk two days east of a hold where they were known, and pick up something that somebody had left lying, and carry it home. He could find no opponent in it. There was a ring on a road, and a rule with no reason given, and instruments, which were only things; and he heard himself thinking that, and distrusted it, since he had thought the same about a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:247:"A hundred and eight," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:251:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:149:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:139:"You draw nothing with those till the hundredth day. Not a bow, not a rope, not a lace if you can find somebody else to pull it. They're skin. They're not hide yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:It had ridden there since the ninety-first morning, folded once, between a letter in a narrow upright hand and a leaf out of a ledger. He opened it on his knee, and they heard it again as Pike had given it to a meadow: the recovery, and the instruments, and the rise where a road left its line. A hundred marks, in words, and again in a bracket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:285:"It is a question. It is put to this hillside, and the hillside cannot write. So it is put to whoever will walk here for a hundred marks; and there is a clerk at a landing, with ink on his cuffs, who has been told to see what is brought down and send word of it west by the next boat." He lowered the paper. "The word that he sends does not need to have a brass level in it. It needs only to say what became of the crew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:253:Brom had never held one, as Cael saw from how he turned it over; but he had drawn carts and hauled rope all his life, and he found the iron stirrup at its nose, and put his boot in that, and bent and took the string in the fingers of both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:309:There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:69:He was at a hundred and eight. That was nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:So he walked back down the grass with two letters, and thirty people watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:Cael folded the sheet along its creases. He had two things now, and he laid them side by side in his head, as Karis had once laid a noun beside six numbers. Somebody above a registry had asked which way certain people had gone; and a paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, written by a hand that had learned its trade at a ruled desk, sending whoever took it two days east of a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:It was no bigger than a grain of barley; it caught, and the flame came up round it, ordinary and smoking, and the seed was gone into it, and Karis let out her breath in something that was not quite a laugh. He had last seen her do that across sixty yards of mountain, with her hands shaking like a rope under load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:It lay in the air a breath. Lira was looking at the stones, and Brom had the bill over his shoulder and was counting his turfs. Oryn, who had stood at that stone since first light, was watching five people come toward her along a line that she could not see. Nobody took the words up, and Karis did not seem to look for anyone to do so. She had entered a property of the figure, and she bent to tie the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:"Ten cords," she said at last. Her voice was not altogether steady. "I worked it at the stone from the round, with a pencil. And I have walked it now with a rope, from the edge, in a straight line, and there is not a full stride between the two. I have never in my life had a figure come home like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:45:Round the foot of the stone, a hand above the floor, there ran a band a hand wide, and in the band there were lines. They were sunk in the grey to the depth of a fingernail, fine and even, some straight and some hooked, some crossing others at a slant; they went right round and met themselves. He walked to the next stone, the one no higher than a knee, and it had its band too. All eleven had. Cael looked for a letter anywhere in it, and found none that he knew, and he could not have sworn that they were letters at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:7:"It's a cord now," she said, coiling it. "It was two ropes, and they are not to be thought of as ropes again until I have done with them. Nobody is to tie a box with this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:63:That night, the ninety-seventh, Lira lay on her back with one arm tied across her chest and found that she could not get comfortable round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:119:Cael had the last watch, on the ninety-eighth morning, and so he was the only one who saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:155:They walked west on the ninety-eighth, and Lira went the whole way on the flat of her foot, since she had been told to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:269:It had opened on the ninety-sixth evening, in a ditch, with nothing under his soles. This was the middle of the ninety-ninth day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:313:She took the first band at his breastbone, where its end was, and drew it off round his side to the spine in one pull, as she had laid it on. It came with a sound like a page tearing. The other four followed. He had expected to feel something give, and he felt only cold air on a place that had not had any for eighteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:323:"You said they'd be done on the ninety-ninth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:"I said the rule was twenty-one days. I'd have laid money on sooner, and I don't lay money." She was rolling the dead tape onto its peeled stick. "Leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:3:It was that same night, the ninety-sixth going into the ninety-seventh, and Seln had the south.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:139:"You draw nothing with those till the hundredth day. Not a bow, not a rope, not a lace if you can find somebody else to pull it. They're skin. They're not hide yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:141:"The hundredth," said Brom. "That's three days. I'll know when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:57:*Eighty-four.* It was passing his feet when she said it, and something in his feet turned over like a sleeper who has heard his name. It was Wind. He did not tell it no, and he did not let it rise. He let it turn over. It settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:59:"The small one runs hot. I felt the heat of hers before I'd found her wrist, on that mountain, and she was empty as a dry well at the time. That's an Ember. The bed's narrow and it's warm to the hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:"And the girl's goes by so fast that there's nothing to feel but the draught of it. You put your hand in and it's gone past already. That's what a Wind is like, under the hands. I had hold of hers for an hour at that stone and I'd not swear that I ever touched the bottom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:101:It was in the thirteenth minute that he understood what was strange about her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:She let herself have that, for the length of a few breaths, since nobody else was going to give it to her. Twenty yards on a thing as round as a rail, in a boot like a bucket, on a leg that she had been forbidden to run on, with two dogs under her heels and one beside her knee; and she had been pulling away from all three. There had been nothing in it but legs. She had felt thirteen. She had felt the lanes at Fenmark come up under her as if they had only been waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:The bead went out. He lay down between two sacks of barley with the book under his hand; and in the dark Wind leaned a little toward where Lira was breathing, and the heaviest of the nine shifted, like a sea a long way off, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:247:Nine things lay in him, and he went along them as he went along anything at the start. Wind was in his feet, and still. Pressure sat under the breastbone. The read was shut, as it had been since a rut. Compression lay in his frame, and Ember in his right hand, with nothing to look at. The bright quick one had asked about the table's edge when he lay down, and Anchor about the sill, and he had told each of them no. The heaviest lay low, like weather that has blown itself out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:107:He could let Wind have him. It would crack like a whip over the lane and set him down beside her with his feet locked for a count, and it would bring eight animals to the ground where Karis was lying, with him standing in the middle of them unable to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:169:Ember was next. He gave it the one place where he could carry it, which was the tip of Lira's knife; and for the first time since the Line he did not stop at the setting. It lit. A seed of yellow stood on the steel, and then a flame as long as his thumb, burning on nothing, at the end of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:189:There was one thing more that he had refused at every doorway and coping since a river, and he let that off its hold with the rest. Anchor fixed nothing, for he had no point on that hill that he could afford to nail to the ground. It only looked, west, as a dog let off a chain looks first at the gate. It would be within ten feet of a thing before it could tell him what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:239:He gave Wind his feet a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:251:It hit him at the end of the count, and Compression took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:275:It was Anchor. It had gone the whole length of that slope with its face to the west and found nothing that it could be sure of. Now it found nine things. They stood in a row directly across his road, a little more than a stride apart, to the right hand and the left, low on the ground, just beyond the stone. Not one of them gave any light. They were places that somebody had fixed and told to stay, as a bar of iron had been told to stay on that same line, three cords to the north. He knew the hand that had set them as he would have known its writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:57:He had told the boy that he did not know what the number counted, and that he would not guess. Both of those were true. There was a third thing which he had not said, because it was not yet a thing: something in him had answered to that iron, and it was not the Shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:175:Wind stood up in his own feet to go with her. He put it down, and it was like putting down his own heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:Everything that he carried had come up, as he had not felt it come since a mountain. Compression showed him the skull under his palms and how little it would take. Wind wanted his feet, and hers. And Ember had the place already, as it had had it once in a ford: he was looking into a yellow eye from a foot away, and it would set a point on anything that he looked at for long. *There.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:271:Cael did not move his hands. He had made the thing as still as he knew how to make it, and that was the whole of what he had to give. Nine fragments lay under his refusal, and not one of them could do what was wanted, which was to let a friend try. He looked across thirteen feet at one grey eye over a bow-stock, narrowed, and at a face that would have done anything on earth sooner than loose that string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:*Nine hounds, inside. Two dead, both Brom's, with a healer's bow that he had never shot: one on the floor, at thirteen feet, with my hands on its head; one over the stone, at six. Seven out, north and south along the line's outside. Not seen after. No instruments were there. No price.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:205:*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:207:*Wind: a burst, north off the lane. The lock.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:*Ember: one point, on a knife, lane to stone. Nothing seen beyond ten feet of it. A blister where the hilt was.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:*Blade: once. An edge on that knife. The knife won't cut wool.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:215:*Anchor: looking, nothing fixed. It showed me nine points before they lit.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:217:*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:219:*Wind: the second burst. The lock had a hound in it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:221:*Compression: in that lock, late. That's the arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:225:*Twelve. With the ford, thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*

