122:## New canon minted (flag where marked)
124:- **The pack came home by the west (flag).** Nine came over the floor's western lip at sunset, up the party's own trodden lane, having gone out by the east at first light. Cael notes that a healer counted to ten on a wrist at that stone at noon with her Path running; "he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings." It is not entered as a finding by anybody.
126:- **The rain-rut (flag):** natural, on the swell's north-west flank; it begins under the floor's north-western lip and runs down a little north of west, knee-deep and a stride wide; abreast of the lane's sixty-yard mark it lies about forty yards north. Nothing made is added to the site.
127:- **How the hounds behave inside (flag):** careless. They drift; they keep station on the slowest walker; they do not cut a lane that they could cut; one sits down in the lane behind the file. One comes onto a pole a minute after watching another do it. A man who cannot declare is "a man with a stick" to them. A scream turns no head. With their first mover dead, two stand. Once something declares inside, they come as the ford's seven came.
129:- **Oryn's crossbow (flag):** has an iron stirrup at its nose and is spanned with a hook, which was in the near-side pannier and was not fetched; drawn by hand it cuts the fingers. It "throws low, a hand" at thirteen feet. A leather sleeve on the stock held **six** bolts. No history of the bow is given; Oryn says only that she looked at it at noon and left it, "a healer's reason".
135:- **What the reading is like from under it (flag):** it goes along the length of a person and returns to her other hand, at the pace she walks, favouring the right by a hair. He can tell where she is. As she passes: Wind turns over and settles; his palm warms; Compression does nothing; the read wishes to open; the ninth draws itself in small, unasked; the heaviest lies quiet. She says "You're helping." when his neck hardens.
137:- **Seln's memory (flag):** as a junior he carried a tin with a lead tag from one room to another and counted, without reading, eight fair linen-backed sheets of the country east of the Line: a ruled stroke for the Line, dashed roads carried farther than on the printed sheet, and on some sheets a small round ruled with a compass, empty, with a road going about one; no sign for it in the table at the sheet's foot; one sheet with two. He took them for something guarded. No room, office, year or name is given, and he says that he cannot place the second round.
